    <body>
      <sec id="article-139206.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Parry-Romberg syndrome, or progressive hemifacial atrophy, is a rare, chronic, and multifactorial disorder characterized by progressive unilateral atrophy of facial tissues, including skin, subcutaneous fat, muscle, cartilage, and bone. Typically manifesting during the first or second decade of life, Parry-Romberg syndrome progresses slowly over several years before stabilizing. This course reviews the neurological, ophthalmologic, dental, and dermatologic manifestations that frequently accompany the facial changes, reflecting the condition's multisystemic nature. The pathogenesis is not clearly proven. Various proposed mechanisms include autoimmune, neurogenic, and vascular causes, often with overlap syndromes such as localized scleroderma. Early recognition and&#x000a0;interprofessional management are essential to optimize functional, aesthetic, and psychosocial outcomes.</p>
        <p>This course&#x000a0;explores the pathophysiology, clinical manifestations, diagnostic evaluation, and evolving management strategies of Parry-Romberg syndrome. Participants will gain competence in differentiating Parry-Romberg syndrome from similar neurocutaneous and autoimmune disorders, applying appropriate diagnostic imaging and serologic assessments, and implementing evidence-based therapeutic and reconstructive interventions.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in identifying Parry-Romberg syndrome, performing the recommended evaluation, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach when managing this condition&#x000a0;to improve outcomes and quality of life.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify&#x000a0;the clinical manifestations of Parry-Romberg syndrome.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine the diagnostic&#x000a0;criteria for&#x000a0;Parry-Romberg syndrome.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Interpret&#x000a0;the management guidelines for the ocular manifestations of Parry-Romberg syndrome.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with the interprofessional team to achieve successful cosmetic rehabilitation&#x000a0;for&#x000a0;patients with Parry-Romberg syndrome.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=139206&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=139206">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Parry&#x02013;Romberg syndrome (PRS), also known as progressive hemifacial atrophy, progressive facial hemiatrophy, or idiopathic hemifacial atrophy, is an idiopathic, gradually progressive craniofacial asymmetry characterized by atrophy of subcutaneous tissue, muscle, bone, and cartilage on one side of the face. Caleb Hillier Parry first described the disorder in 1815, with further elaboration by Moritz Heinrich Romberg in 1846, giving rise to its eponym.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Unilateral facial wasting, which produces marked asymmetry, defines the disease.</p>
        <p>Although the precise cause remains uncertain, current evidence supports a multifactorial pathogenesis involving autoimmune, neurogenic, and vascular mechanisms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Despite these theories, no unifying mechanism accounts for all clinical presentations, underscoring the heterogeneous nature of PRS.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;One or more dermatomes within the trigeminal nerve distribution are commonly affected, with early ophthalmic and neurologic involvement and variable maxillofacial and cardiac abnormalities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Clinically, PRS often begins with subtle, ill-defined changes such as skin tightening, hyperpigmentation, or localized fat loss on one side of the face, typically in the cheek or periorbital region. Deeper involvement may affect muscle, cartilage, and bone, resulting in severe deformity, while the contralateral side occasionally shows mild compensatory hypertrophy. Ocular manifestations may include enophthalmos from orbital fat loss, uveitis, heterochromia, or retinal vascular changes. Neurologic complications like seizures, trigeminal neuralgia, migraine, and hemiatrophic epilepsy are well-documented.&#x000a0;The psychosocial consequences are profound, particularly among children and adolescents, in whom disfigurement often leads to emotional distress, social withdrawal, anxiety, and reduced self-esteem.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Due to the unpredictable rate of progression and variable extent of involvement, early recognition and coordinated interprofessional management remain essential to achieving optimal functional and cosmetic outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Diagnosis primarily relies on detailed clinical assessment, laboratory testing, and imaging studies such as CT and MRI scans. Histopathologic analysis is rarely performed, but it can help confirm the diagnosis and differentiate PRS from conditions such as Rasmussen encephalitis, linear scleroderma, and posttraumatic fat necrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Systemic associations highlight the disorder&#x02019;s multisystem nature.</p>
        <p>Treatment focuses on halting disease progression and restoring facial symmetry. During the active phase, immunomodulatory therapies are employed with varying success, especially when inflammatory overlap with linear scleroderma is evident. Once stability occurs, reconstructive surgery becomes the cornerstone of management. Advances in 3-dimensional (3D) imaging and surgical planning have enhanced the precision of reconstruction and outcomes. Neurologic and ophthalmologic complications require targeted therapies. Physical therapy and psychological counseling significantly contribute to rehabilitation and emotional well-being, improving quality of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Ongoing research continues to refine the understanding of PRS. Continued research may eventually yield targeted therapies capable of transforming management from symptomatic restoration to a disease-modifying intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The etiopathogenesis of PRS remains elusive, reflecting its heterogeneous clinical presentation and multifactorial biological underpinnings (see <bold>Image</bold>. Parry-Romberg Syndrome, Etiology). Recent immunopathologic and neuroimaging studies indicate that vascular, neural, and immune mechanisms interact synergistically to produce progressive, asymmetric facial hemiatrophy. While several hypotheses have been advanced, ranging from neurogenic and autoimmune to infectious and developmental origins, PRS likely represents a final common pathway of several pathophysiologic insults acting on a susceptible neurocutaneous interface (See <bold>Table 1</bold>).&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Theory of trophoneurosis:&#x000a0;</bold>The earliest understanding of the disease suggested a dysfunction of the trophic fibers of the trigeminal and other peripheral nerves, termed <italic toggle="yes">trophoneurosis</italic>, with&#x000a0;symptoms&#x000a0;of chronic facial pain and trigeminal neuralgia supporting this theory. This theory posits that disruption of trophic neural input leads to progressive atrophy of the skin, subcutaneous tissue, and bone.&#x000a0;MRI tractography has revealed ipsilateral white-matter hyperintensities along the trigeminal pathways, consistent with demyelinating or inflammatory neuropathy. Moreover, sympathetic nervous system dysregulation, especially of the superior cervical ganglion, has been implicated in vasospasm and regional ischemia.&#x000a0;Additionally, confocal microscopy and corneal esthesiometry show a reduction in corneal nerve endings supplied by the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve,&#x000a0;corroborating this theory.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Immune-mediated process:&#x000a0;</bold>The widely accepted theory is that PRS is an autoimmune disorder.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Overlap with linear morphea, a form of systemic sclerosis, has been&#x000a0;commonly observed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;The presence of other autoimmune diseases like systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, inflammatory bowel disease, ankylosing spondylitis, vitiligo, and thyroid disorders has been noted in rare cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;The presence of antinuclear (ANA) and anti-dsDNA antibodies provides a serologic basis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;Among all proposed theories, the autoimmune hypothesis has the strongest empirical support. Histopathologic studies of affected dermis have revealed perivascular and perineural lymphocytic infiltration, endothelial swelling, and deposition of complement (C3) and immunoglobulins. The shared clinicopathologic spectrum between PRS and localized scleroderma suggests that both conditions may represent varying expressions of a common autoimmune process triggered by an external or vascular insult.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neurovasculitis theory:&#x000a0;</bold>The histopathology of the cerebral lesions has revealed a lymphocytic vasculitis, similar to the features seen in Rasmussen encephalitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Interstitial neurovasculitis involving the major vessels&#x000a0;explains the&#x000a0;aneurysms&#x000a0;and other cerebrovascular malformations that are occasionally seen in PRS. Similar lesions are noted along the course of the trigeminal nerve.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;These vascular changes can explain associated neurologic manifestations such as seizures, white matter lesions, and intracranial calcifications. Vasospastic episodes or microangiopathy involving the facial vasculature may compromise the regional blood flow, precipitating progressive hemifacial atrophy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neural crest cell disorder:&#x000a0;</bold>The presence of cerebral vascular malformations, aneurysms, and soft tissue tumors suggests a possible dysregulation of neural crest cell migration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;PRS has been conceptualized as a neurocristopathy (a defect in the migration or differentiation of neural crest cells). Since the neural crest cells give rise to craniofacial bones, facial dermis, and vascular smooth muscles, a defect in this pathway can explain facial hemiatrophy. Developmental dysregulation or mosaicism in neural crest derivatives also explains the frequent coexistence of vascular anomalies, meningeal malformations, and soft tissue tumors. This theory bridges the gap between congenital and acquired models of the disease.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Sympathetic dysfunction:&#x000a0;</bold>Inflammation or dysfunction of the superior cervical ganglion can cause ipsilateral facial atrophy, enophthalmos, and bone atrophy, similar to that seen in PRS. Ipsilateral sympathectomy halts disease progression in PRS.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Sympathetic overactivity or denervation can produce localized ischemia and tissue atrophy identical to that seen in PRS. Experimental animal models of unilateral cervical sympathectomy reproduce ipsilateral facial thinning and bone resorption. Moreover, endocrine disturbances such as hypothyroidism and hypopituitarism, though uncommon, have been reported in some patients, possibly reflecting broader hypothalamic&#x02013;autonomic dysregulation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hereditary mechanism:&#x000a0;</bold>Wartenberg&#x000a0;described PRS as a heredodegenerative disorder, with only a few familial cases noted. No specific inheritance pattern or gene has been isolated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Genetic susceptibility:&#x000a0;</bold>Although most cases of PRS are sporadic, a few familial clusters have been described. However, no definitive genetic inheritance pattern has been identified. Recent genetic studies suggest a polygenic predisposition involving immune regulatory genes and endothelial homeostasis pathways. Whole-exome sequencing in small cohorts has revealed variants in protein tyrosine phosphatase nonreceptor type 22 (<italic toggle="yes">PTPN22)</italic>,&#x000a0;HLA class II histocompatibility antigen, DRB1 &#x003b2;-chain&#x000a0;(<italic toggle="yes">HLA-DRB1)</italic>, and&#x000a0;collagen type V&#x000a0;&#x003b1;-1 chain (<italic toggle="yes">COL5A1)</italic>, but their pathogenicity remains uncertain. The incomplete penetrance and phenotypic variability point toward a multifactorial inheritance influenced by epigenetic modulation and environmental triggers.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Trauma-induced:&#x000a0;</bold>Online surveys of patients with Parry-Romberg syndrome&#x000a0;have established a debatable association between head injury in early childhood and the onset of symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;A subset of patients reports antecedent facial trauma or surgical injury preceding the onset of atrophy. Mechanical damage to small vessels or peripheral nerves may act as a local trigger for autoimmunity, ischemia, or sympathetic dysregulation. However, this correlation does not establish causation. Environmental toxins, exposure to cold, or chronic vasospasm have also been considered contributing factors in sporadic cases.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Infectious causes:&#x000a0;</bold>Several infectious agents have been proposed as potential triggers through immune molecular mimicry or direct neurovascular injury. Infections with varicella zoster virus (VZV), herpes simplex virus (HSV), and <italic toggle="yes">Borrelia burgorferi</italic> have been described as risk factors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;Although not causative, these infections might serve as inciting events in genetically predisposed individuals.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
