      <sec id="article-139206.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The incidence of Parry-Romberg syndrome ranges from 0.3 to 2.5 cases per 100,000 persons per year, with a male-to-female ratio of approximately 1:3.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;The onset of Parry-Romberg syndrome is in the first&#x000a0;2 decades of life, although a few cases have been reported in older adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;The mean age of diagnosis is 13.2 years, with an earlier diagnosis in men.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;The disease exhibits a progressive course, followed by a burnout phase that can occur within 2 to 20 years, and may also result in spontaneous remission.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Ophthalmic involvement occurs in up to 35% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;Neurologic symptoms manifest in 15% to 20%&#x000a0;of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;Global epidemiologic data on&#x000a0;Parry-Romberg syndrome remain limited due to the condition&#x02019;s rarity, variable presentation, and frequent overlap with localized scleroderma. However, multiple regional registries and large tertiary case series have contributed to a clearer understanding of its demographic patterns, incidence, and clinical spectrum (see <bold>Table 2</bold>).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Global Prevalence and Incidence</bold>
</p>
        <p>Parry-Romberg syndrome is estimated to affect approximately 1 in 250,000 to 1 in 1,000,000 individuals worldwide. However, the actual prevalence is likely underreported due to misdiagnosis and clinical overlap with morphea <italic toggle="yes">en coup de sabre.</italic> (see <bold>Image</bold>. Parry-Romberg Syndrome, Epidemiological Distribution). In Europe and North America, data from tertiary dermatologic and ophthalmologic centers suggest that Parry-Romberg syndrome accounts for 2% to 5% of cases of localized scleroderma. In India, Mexico, and other developing regions, the incidence may be higher owing to postinfectious or posttraumatic triggers and increased awareness of autoimmune neurocutaneous syndromes. No specific racial or ethnic predilection has been consistently demonstrated; however, a slightly higher reporting frequency is observed among White individuals, possibly reflecting better diagnostic documentation rather than actual biological variation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Age Distribution</bold>
</p>
        <p>The onset of Parry-Romberg syndrome typically occurs during the first and second decades of life, with 80% to 90% of cases beginning before age 20. The mean age of onset across studies is 8 to 18 years, though the clinical manifestations often evolve slowly over several years before diagnosis. Pediatric onset is more common, but adult presentations, particularly after age 25, have also been reported and are often associated with milder or localized forms. The disease progression usually stabilizes within 2 to 10 years after onset, though a minority of patients experience relapsing or slowly progressive disease into adulthood. In children, the earlier onset often correlates with greater skeletal deformity due to interference with facial bone growth. In contrast, adult-onset Parry-Romberg syndrome tends to produce more soft-tissue atrophy without significant osseous remodeling.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Sex Distribution</bold>
</p>
        <p>Nearly all large series report a female predominance, with a female-to-male ratio ranging from 2:1 to 3:1. This pattern parallels the epidemiologic behavior of autoimmune diseases, further supporting an immunologic etiology. In a 2022 multicenter European cohort of 133 patients with Parry-Romberg syndrome and localized morphea overlap, 68% of the patients were women. Similarly, in a North American registry-based study, the female predominance was 71%, consistent with autoimmune bias across connective tissue diseases. Despite female predominance, men often present with earlier onset and more severe neuro-ophthalmologic involvement, including seizures, trigeminal neuropathy, and ocular changes (eg, enophthalmos and uveitis).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Laterality and Anatomic Distribution</bold>
</p>
        <p>Parry-Romberg syndrome overwhelmingly demonstrates unilateral involvement, with left-sided facial atrophy in nearly 60% to 75% of cases. Bilateral disease is rare, reported in 5% to 10% of patients, and typically shows asymmetric progression. The distribution of atrophy usually follows dermatomal boundaries of the trigeminal nerve (V1&#x02013;V3 divisions). However, in advanced disease, the process may extend beyond these zones to involve the neck, scalp, tongue, and occasionally the contralateral face. Ocular and orbital involvement (enophthalmos, eyelid retraction, retinal vasculitis) occurs in 25% to 40% of patients, while oral and dental abnormalities occur in approximately 20% to 30%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Geographic Variations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Although the syndrome has been reported globally, epidemiologic clusters have emerged in certain regions, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Europe and North America:</bold> Predominant autoimmune association and overlap with morphea <italic toggle="yes">en coup de sabre</italic></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Asia (India, Japan, China):</bold> Higher rates of infectious or postinflammatory triggers</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>South America:</bold> Pediatric cohorts with early-onset and greater neurologic involvement</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Africa:</bold> Limited reports, possibly due to underrecognition or diagnostic limitations&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r26">[26]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Environmental and geographic differences may influence triggers such as viral infections, exposure to <italic toggle="yes">Borrelia </italic>spp, or the frequency of trauma, thereby altering regional epidemiologic trends.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Associated Neurological and Systemic Manifestations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Approximately 15% to 20% of patients with Parry-Romberg syndrome develop neurologic complications, including focal epilepsy, migraine-like headaches, hemiparesis, and cranial neuropathies. Neuroimaging studies, including MRI and magnetic resonance angiography, demonstrate ipsilateral cortical atrophy, white-matter hyperintensities, and vascular malformations. A smaller subset (&#x0003c;5%) exhibits systemic autoimmune overlap with systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, or thyroiditis, consistent with a multisystemic immune etiology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Pediatric Versus Adult Disease&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>In children, the onset is often abrupt, with rapid progression during the first few years, followed by a plateau phase. Bone hypoplasia, dental malalignment, and maxillary retrusion are frequent due to disruption of growth centers. In contrast, adults experience insidious soft-tissue atrophy without skeletal distortion. The mean disease duration until stabilization averages 7.2 years in children, compared with 4.8 years in adults. Notably, neurologic comorbidities are more common in childhood-onset disease, while psychosocial impact and cosmetic disfigurement predominate in adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>United States Epidemiological&#x000a0;Overview</bold>
</p>
        <p>In the United States (US), the estimated prevalence is 0.3 per 100,000, with roughly 1 to 2 new cases diagnosed per million persons annually. Data from the National Organization for Rare Disorders and from tertiary centers such as the Mayo Clinic and Johns Hopkins corroborate this rarity. Patients with Parry-Romberg syndrome often present to dermatologists or ophthalmologists first, which can delay diagnosis by an average of 3 years.&#x000a0;Interprofessional referral networks have improved early recognition; however, underreporting persists, particularly in patients with mild or segmental forms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r28">[28]</xref></p>
