      <sec id="article-139206.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Parry-Romberg syndrome is predominantly an autoimmune disorder with similarities to localized scleroderma (morphea). The closest&#x000a0;mimic is linear scleroderma <italic toggle="yes">en coup de sabre</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;The primary distinguishing feature is the absence of cicatricial alopecia and skin induration in patients with Parry-Romberg syndrome. In contrast,&#x000a0;linear scleroderma&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">en coup de sabre</italic> demonstrates scalp cutaneous sclerosis, hyperpigmentation, and loss of scalp hair and eyebrows.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Autoimmune Dysregulation and Immune-Mediated Inflammation</bold>
</p>
        <p>The most consistent finding across recent studies is the role of autoimmunity. Parry-Romberg syndrome demonstrates immune-mediated destruction of mesenchymal&#x000a0;tissue within a defined facial segment (see&#x000a0;<bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Parry-Romberg Syndrome, Pathophysiology).&#x000a0;Histopathological examination reveals lymphocytic and plasma cell infiltration within the dermis, subcutis, and perivascular regions, accompanied by endothelial damage and perivascular fibrosis. This inflammatory reaction mirrors the changes seen in morphea, strengthening the concept that Parry-Romberg syndrome is an immune-mediated panniculitis and vasculopathy rather than a primary neurodegenerative disorder.</p>
        <p>Autoantibodies (ANA, anti&#x02013;dsDNA, and anti-centromere antibodies) are detected in approximately 25% to 40% of patients, particularly those with concurrent localized scleroderma. The inflammatory cascade involves&#x000a0;the upregulation of&#x000a0;T-helper (Th1 and Th17) cytokines, including interleukin (IL)-2, IL-6, IL-17, and tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-&#x003b1;, leading to fibroblast activation, collagen overproduction, and tissue sclerosis. The progression from inflammation to atrophy may be attributed to immune-mediated microvascular occlusion and subsequent ischemic tissue necrosis, ultimately resulting in the characteristic facial atrophy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Neurogenic and Trigeminal Pathway Involvement</bold>
</p>
        <p>The classic trophoneurosis theory, first proposed in the 19th century, postulates that degeneration of trigeminal and facial nerve trophic fibers leads to localized autonomic and metabolic imbalance in the affected dermatome, which remains a cornerstone in understanding Parry-Romberg syndrome. Modern imaging corroborates this: high-resolution MRI and diffusion tensor imaging demonstrate demyelination and asymmetric white matter loss along trigeminal sensory nuclei, brainstem, and internal capsule in up to 30% of patients. Confocal microscopy has&#x000a0;demonstrated&#x000a0;a reduction in corneal nerve fiber density&#x000a0;in the ophthalmic division (V1), which correlates&#x000a0;with the neurotrophic ulceration and corneal hypoesthesia occasionally observed&#x000a0;in Parry-Romberg syndrome. These findings&#x000a0;suggest that&#x000a0;neurogenic inflammation&#x000a0;may act as both a trigger and a perpetuating mechanism, linking neural injury to&#x000a0;subsequent microvascular dysfunction and tissue hypoperfusion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Vascular and Neurovasculitic Mechanisms</bold>
</p>
        <p>Histologic and radiologic studies&#x000a0;demonstrate strong evidence for&#x000a0;lymphocytic vasculitis&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;microangiopathy, suggesting that vascular dysfunction plays a role in the progression of Parry-Romberg syndrome. Endothelial swelling, perivascular lymphocytic cuffing, and vascular lumen narrowing are consistent features on biopsy. Cerebral MRI often reveals ipsilateral white matter hyperintensities, cortical atrophy, and meningeal enhancement consistent with chronic vasculitic injury. Several patients with neurologic manifestations (eg, seizures or hemiparesis) exhibit findings resembling Rasmussen encephalitis, a unilateral, immune-mediated cerebral vasculitis. These vascular lesions compromise nutrient and oxygen delivery, leading to progressive ischemia and soft-tissue apoptosis, explaining the stepwise deterioration and stabilization phases typical of Parry-Romberg syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Sympathetic Nervous System Dysfunction</bold>
</p>
        <p>Sympathetic hyperactivity or inflammation of the superior cervical ganglion has been proposed as another pathophysiologic mechanism for Parry-Romberg syndrome. Experimental sympathectomy in animal models reproduces features resembling Parry-Romberg syndrome, such as facial hypoperfusion, enophthalmos, and bone resorption. The sympathetic fibers regulate regional blood flow, collagen metabolism, and melanocyte activity; thus, their dysfunction leads to vasoconstriction-induced ischemia, skin depigmentation, and subcutaneous fat atrophy. Clinical reports of disease arrest following ipsilateral cervical sympathectomy in refractory disease lends indirect support to this theory.&#x000a0;This mechanism also aligns with the observation that atrophy respects dermatomal boundaries and ceases once sympathetic remodeling stabilizes, years after its initial progression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Neural Crest Dysgenesis and Developmental Mechanisms</bold>
</p>
        <p>Another proposed theory considers Parry-Romberg syndrome a mosaic neurocristopathy, arising from localized dysregulation in neural crest cell migration and differentiation during embryogenesis. Since neural crest cells give rise to craniofacial mesenchyme, Schwann cells, and melanocytes, a postzygotic mutation or epigenetic modification could lead to segmental tissue susceptibility. This developmental vulnerability might explain why the disease often follows strict midline demarcation and sometimes coexists with vascular malformations or hemangiomas. Although no definitive gene mutation has been identified, mosaic somatic alterations in immune-regulatory or collagen-synthesis pathways are under investigation through next-generation sequencing of affected tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Infectious Triggers and Postinfectious Immune Activation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Several infections have been temporally associated with the onset of Parry-Romberg syndrome. <italic toggle="yes">Borrelia burgdorferi</italic> DNA has been isolated from skin biopsies in some European patients, suggesting an infectious-immune trigger analogous to localized scleroderma. Viral infections with herpes simplex and varicella zoster may also incite localized inflammation and vasculitis through molecular mimicry or immune cross-reactivity. However, these infections are considered triggers rather than primary causes, acting in individuals who are genetically or immunologically predisposed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r35">[35]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Genetic and Epigenetic Susceptibility</bold>
</p>
        <p>While Parry-Romberg syndrome is generally sporadic, rare familial cases suggest a polygenic or epigenetic predisposition. Aberrant methylation of genes regulating extracellular matrix turnover (eg, collagen type I &#x003b1;1<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>[<italic toggle="yes">COL1A1], </italic>transforming growth factor &#x003b2;-1 [<italic toggle="yes">TGFB1]</italic>) and immune modulation (<italic toggle="yes">HLA-DRB1, </italic>cytotoxic T-lymphocyte-associated protein 4<italic toggle="yes"> [CTLA4]</italic>) has been implicated. Epigenetic silencing of microRNAs (eg, miR-29b and miR-21), which are known to regulate fibrosis and immune responses, has been demonstrated in localized scleroderma and may extend to the pathogenesis of Parry-Romberg syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r36">[36]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Pathophysiologic Sequence</bold>
</p>
        <p>Integrating all evidence, the disease likely&#x000a0;follows&#x000a0;the following&#x000a0;multistep model (see <bold>Image</bold>. Parry Romberg Syndrome Pathophysiology):</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Trigger:</bold>&#x000a0;Trauma, infection, or microvascular injury</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Immune activation:</bold>&#x000a0;Autoantibody production, T-cell infiltration</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neurovascular inflammation:</bold>&#x000a0;Trigeminal or sympathetic nerve dysfunction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ischemic atrophy:</bold>&#x000a0;Endothelial damage leading to tissue hypoperfusion</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Fibrosis and stabilization:</bold>&#x000a0;Residual scarring, dermal sclerosis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r37">[37]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>This sequential model explains the classic natural history of Parry-Romberg syndrome, characterized by&#x000a0;slow unilateral progression, a plateau phase, and eventual arrest.</p>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>Table 3. </bold>Proposed Mechanistic Pathways in Parry-Romberg Syndrome</p>
