      <sec id="article-139206.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Skin biopsy shows&#x000a0;near-complete dermal fat atrophy, minimal sclerosis, and reduced adnexal structures. The characteristic finding is a&#x000a0;diffuse dermal lymphocytic infiltration with perivascular plasma cells.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;Brain biopsy, although not routinely performed for Parry-Romberg syndrome, reveals lymphocytic interstitial vasculitis.</p>
        <p>Histopathologic findings in Parry-Romberg Syndrome reflect a combination of chronic inflammatory, vascular, and atrophic changes affecting the skin, subcutaneous fat, muscle, and sometimes bone (see <bold>Image</bold>. Parry-Romberg Syndrome, Histological Findings). These features vary with disease stage; inflammatory vasculitis predominates in early stages, and atrophic fibrosis in late, quiescent phases. Histopathology remains the gold standard for differentiating Parry-Romberg syndrome from localized scleroderma and other causes of facial hemiatrophy (see <bold>Table 4</bold>).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Epidermal and Dermal Changes</bold>
</p>
        <p>The epidermis is usually thinned and flattened, with loss of rete ridges and reduced basal pigmentation. Occasionally, epidermal atrophy is minimal, while the dermis shows marked sclerosis and loss of adnexal structures, including sebaceous and sweat glands. Dermal collagen bundles are thickened, eosinophilic, and arranged parallel to the epidermal surface, resembling those in morphea.</p>
        <p>The dermal appendages may be replaced by dense fibrous tissue, resulting in a clinically smooth, shiny, and depigmented skin surface. In active lesions, a perivascular lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate extends around small and medium-sized vessels, often accompanied by endothelial swelling and narrowing of the vascular lumen. As the disease stabilizes, the inflammatory infiltrate diminishes, giving way to&#x000a0;fibroblast proliferation&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;the deposition of hyalinized collagen, which&#x000a0;leads&#x000a0;to permanent dermal atrophy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Subcutaneous Fat and Panniculus</bold>
</p>
        <p>Loss of subcutaneous fat is a hallmark feature of Parry-Romberg syndrome. Histologically, the subcutis&#x000a0;exhibits&#x000a0;lobular panniculitis,&#x000a0;characterized by adipocyte destruction, hyalinization of interlobular septa, and variable infiltration by lymphocytes and histiocytes. Over time, adipose tissue is replaced by fibrous connective tissue, merging indistinctly with the dermis above and the underlying fascia below. The fat lobules appear collapsed, with ghost-like adipocytes, suggesting ischemic degeneration. Calcification or lipogranulomatous reaction indicates long-standing necrosis.&#x000a0;This fat loss contributes significantly to the characteristic contour deformity and sunken appearance of the affected hemiface.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r40">[40]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Vascular Changes</bold>
</p>
        <p>One of the most consistent histopathologic findings in Parry-Romberg syndrome is lymphocytic vasculitis. Arterioles and venules exhibit perivascular mononuclear infiltration, endothelial proliferation, and fibrinoid degeneration of the vessel wall. The lumen may be narrowed or occluded, leading to microvascular ischemia. In advanced stages, the vessels show intimal thickening, hyaline change, and perivascular fibrosis. These changes underlie the ischemic atrophy of overlying tissues and correlate clinically with progression arrest when vascular obliteration stabilizes. Electron microscopy&#x000a0;reveals&#x000a0;basement-membrane thickening&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;perivascular collagen deposition, similar to that observed in&#x000a0;localized scleroderma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Muscular and Fascial Changes</bold>
</p>
        <p>The skeletal muscle fibers underlying the affected area are atrophic, angulated, and fragmented, with interstitial fibrosis replacing muscle fascicles. Muscle fibers show diameter variation, sarcoplasmic eosinophilia, and nuclear internalization, suggesting chronic denervation. Perimysial inflammation may be observed, reflecting secondary myositis due to neurovascular compromise. In&#x000a0;the late stages,&#x000a0;fibrofatty replacement of muscle&#x000a0;occurs, resulting in&#x000a0;near-total loss of contractile tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r42">[42]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Osseous and Cartilaginous Changes</bold>
</p>
        <p>In long-standing disease, the maxillary and mandibular bones exhibit osteoclastic resorption, cortical thinning, and decreased trabecular density. Histologic sections show osteopenia, bone marrow fibrosis, and reduced osteoblast activity, consistent with chronic hypoperfusion. In children, premature closure of the epiphyseal plates on the affected side may cause facial asymmetry and growth retardation. Cartilaginous tissues such as the nasal septum and auricular cartilage exhibit&#x000a0;atrophy and fibrosis, contributing to nasal and auricular deformities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r43">[43]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Neural and Perineural Involvement</bold>
</p>
        <p>Histologic and immunohistochemical studies reveal perineural and endoneurial inflammation accompanied by&#x000a0;loss of myelinated fibers and fibrotic replacement. In some biopsies, vasculitic changes within perineural vessels have been&#x000a0;observed, supporting the hypothesis that&#x000a0;neurovasculitis&#x000a0;is&#x000a0;a key pathogenic mechanism. These neural changes explain the frequent association of facial pain, trigeminal neuralgia, and sensory disturbances in patients with Parry-Romberg syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r44">[44]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Cerebral and Meningeal Correlates</bold>
</p>
        <p>In patients with neurologic manifestations, brain biopsy or imaging reveals lymphocytic meningoencephalitis, microglial activation, and gliosis within the ipsilateral cerebral hemisphere. These features overlap with Rasmussen encephalitis, supporting a shared immune-mediated vasculitic mechanism. Meningeal vessels may exhibit focal fibrinoid necrosis, while cortical tissue demonstrates neuronal loss and astrocytic proliferation. These findings correlate with seizures and focal neurological deficits observed clinically.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r45">[45]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Immunohistochemistry and Molecular Findings</bold>
</p>
        <p>Immunohistochemical staining in active lesions highlights CD4<sup>+</sup> and CD8<sup>+</sup> T-cell infiltration, with CD68<sup>+</sup> macrophages and occasional CD20<sup>+&#x000a0;</sup>B cells. Increased expression of transforming growth factor &#x003b2; (TGF-&#x003b2;), collagen type I, and fibronectin supports ongoing fibrosis. Endothelial cells stain positively for vascular cell adhesion molecule-1 and intracellular adhesion molecule-1, indicating endothelial activation and leukocyte adhesion. Results from recent studies using polymerase chain reaction have detected <italic toggle="yes">Borrelia burgdorferi</italic> DNA in a subset of European patients, though causality remains unproven. Ultrastructural analysis also reveals mitochondrial swelling and perivascular basement-membrane duplication, signifying chronic hypoxia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r46">[46]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 4. Histopathologic Features of Parry-Romberg Syndrome</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
