        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s7" sec-type="Toxicokinetics">
        <title>Toxicokinetics</title>
        <p>Although Parry-Romberg Syndrome is primarily a neurocutaneous and autoimmune disorder, the concept of toxicokinetics becomes relevant when examining the disease&#x02019;s molecular and biochemical milieu. Toxicokinetics, in this context, refers not to an exogenous toxin per se but rather to the endogenous accumulation, metabolism, and clearance of neurotoxic and immunotoxic mediators that contribute to the progressive atrophy and neural dysfunction observed in Parry-Romberg syndrome (see <bold>Table 5</bold>). The interplay of immune dysregulation, oxidative stress, and neurovascular impairment creates a pathophysiologic environment&#x000a0;resembling a toxic insult to neural and connective tissues (see&#x000a0;<bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Toxicokinetics of Parry-Romberg Syndrome).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r47">[47]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Endogenous Neurotoxins and Inflammatory Mediators</bold>
</p>
        <p>In Parry-Romberg syndrome, lymphocytic vasculitis and neuroinflammation lead to the release of neurotoxic cytokines, including&#x000a0;tumor necrosis factor-&#x003b1; (TNF-&#x003b1;), interleukin-6 (IL-6), and interferon-&#x003b3; (IFN-&#x003b3;). These mediators alter the permeability of the blood&#x02013;nerve and blood&#x02013;brain barriers, allowing further infiltration of activated lymphocytes and macrophages. The accumulation of reactive oxygen species and nitric oxide causes oxidative injury to neurons, Schwann cells, and the endothelial lining, leading to demyelination and tissue necrosis. These locally generated reactive intermediates&#x000a0;function as&#x000a0;endogenous toxins,&#x000a0;perpetuating&#x000a0;vascular spasm, ischemia, and progressive loss of facial tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r48">[48]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Autoimmune Toxicodynamics</bold>
</p>
        <p>The autoimmune component of Parry-Romberg syndrome behaves similarly to toxicokinetic injury, driven by self-sustaining immune activation. Autoantibodies, such as ANA and antiendothelial antibodies, bind to microvascular endothelium, triggering complement activation and leukocyte recruitment. The resulting immune complex triggers a toxic cascade of endothelial apoptosis and microvascular occlusion, mimicking the chronic vascular toxicity observed in systemic autoimmune diseases such as systemic sclerosis. Over time, immune complex deposition and cytokine overproduction create a chronic immune toxic environment that damages both soft and hard tissues.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r49">[49]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Role of Microvascular Ischemic Toxemia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Endothelial dysfunction in Parry-Romberg syndrome impairs nitric oxide signaling, leading to chronic microvascular ischemia and the accumulation of metabolic byproducts, such as lactate, free radicals, and lipid peroxidation products.&#x000a0;These metabolites act as localized toxins, promoting fibrosis and apoptosis of fibroblasts, adipocytes, and myocytes. The impaired clearance of ischemic metabolites in atrophic regions further accelerates tissue degeneration. Electron microscopy demonstrates mitochondrial swelling, vacuolization, and perivascular collagen deposition consistent with oxidative toxic injury.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r50">[50]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Infectious Neurotoxin Hypothesis</bold>
</p>
        <p>A subset of patients with Parry-Romberg syndrome has been found to harbor DNA fragments of <italic toggle="yes">Borrelia burgdorferi</italic> or herpes simplex virus that can produce neurotoxic lipoproteins and viral proteins that induce persistent immune activation and vasculitis. Although causality remains unproven, these microbial toxins may initiate an autoimmune cascade or directly impair neural metabolism. The borrelial neurotoxins, in particular, have been linked to endothelial injury and perivascular inflammation in experimental models, mirroring the histopathology of Parry-Romberg syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r51">[51]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Neural Degeneration and Excitotoxicity</bold>
</p>
        <p>The involvement of the trigeminal and sympathetic nervous systems in Parry-Romberg syndrome contributes to excitotoxic nerve damage mediated by excessive glutamate and calcium influx. This mechanism parallels toxic neuropathies seen in metabolic or autoimmune diseases. Decreased clearance of neurotransmitter metabolites due to vascular compromise leads to the accumulation of neurotoxic intermediates,&#x000a0;damaging myelin and axons. Chronic sympathetic overactivity can also cause norepinephrine-mediated vasoconstriction, aggravating ischemia and neuronal death.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r52">[52]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Pharmacologic Toxicokinetics </bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients with Parry-Romberg syndrome may be treated with immunosuppressive agents (eg, methotrexate, corticosteroids, cyclophosphamide) or anticonvulsants. Understanding drug toxicokinetics is crucial in these patients, as hepatic and renal metabolism of such drugs may influence disease course or exacerbate existing oxidative stress. Methotrexate can elevate homocysteine levels, further impairing endothelial function, while corticosteroids can alter wound healing and vascular integrity. Monitoring of serum liver enzymes, serum creatinine, and oxidative stress biomarkers (malondialdehyde, glutathione, and superoxide dismutase) is recommended in long-term therapy to prevent additive toxicity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Environmental and Epigenetic Toxic Influences</bold>
</p>
        <p>Although no definitive environmental toxin has been implicated, results from case-control analyses have suggested a possible role for early-life exposure to heavy metals, pesticides, or viral infections in the development of autoimmune vasculopathy in genetically susceptible individuals. Epigenetic mechanisms, including dysregulation of DNA methylation and changes in microRNA expression, may also perpetuate inflammatory signaling and neural injury, functioning as molecular toxicokinetic modifiers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r53">[53]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Integrated Pathophysiologic Model</bold>
</p>
        <p>The toxicokinetic framework in Parry-Romberg syndrome integrates immune toxicity, oxidative stress, ischemic hypoxia, and neurovascular dysfunction into the following cycle:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Initiation phase:</bold> Infection, trauma, or an immune trigger causes endothelial injury</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Propagation phase:</bold> Release of inflammatory cytokines and free radicals damages tissues</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Amplification phase:</bold>&#x000a0;Accumulation of metabolic toxins and immune complexes exacerbates ischemia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Resolution or fibrosis phase:</bold> Cellular death, fibrosis, and atrophy become irreversible&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r4">[4]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>This toxicokinetic cycle underscores that Parry-Romberg syndrome is not merely a structural disorder but a dynamic process of progressive neurovascular and metabolic toxicity leading to hemiatrophy.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
