      <sec id="article-139206.s8" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical Features&#x000a0;of Parry-Romberg Syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>The clinical criteria favoring a diagnosis of Parry-Romberg syndrome over scleroderma&#x000a0;(as proposed by Duymaz et al) are the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Unilateral facial atrophy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Absent&#x000a0;or minimal signs of prior inflammation or induration</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Skin atrophy with thin, soft skin and no sclerosis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r10">[10]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The clinical features that should prompt suspicion of Parry-Romberg syndrome during clinical examination (see <bold>Table 6</bold>) include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Facial findings:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p><bold>Facial atrophy:</bold>&#x000a0;Progressive facial asymmetry due to atrophy of the subcutaneous fat, muscles, and underlying bones (the most common clinical finding)&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Pigmentation:</bold> Hypopigmented or hyperpigmented lesions on the face and ipsilateral neck</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Alopecia:</bold> Involving the eyebrow and the eyelashes</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ophthalmic findings:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p><bold>Enophthalmos: </bold>Progressive enophthalmos secondary to atrophy of retrobulbar fat and orbital bone atrophy</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Eyelid alterations:</bold> Pseudoptosis secondary to enophthalmos, eyelid retraction, lagophthalmos, eyelid atrophy, and late-onset eyelid pseudocoloboma&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r54">[54]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Orbital tumors:</bold> Orbital neurinoma (an incidental MRI finding)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r55">[55]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Extraocular muscle changes:</bold> Thinning or fibrosis of extraocular muscle, sudden-onset horizontal or vertical strabismus, nystagmus, and diplopia</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Conjunctival pigmentation:</bold>&#x000a0;Abnormal pigmentation of the palpebral conjunctiva</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Scleral finding:</bold>&#x000a0;Spontaneous scleral melt&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r56">[56]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Corneal manifestations:</bold> Reduced corneal sensations, reduced stromal nerve fibers on confocal microscopy, exposure keratopathy with secondary infective keratitis, keratitic precipitates, primary endothelial failure, stromal edema, band-shaped keratopathy, and flour-like deposits in the corneal stroma <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r57">[57]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Uveal changes:</bold> Acute uveitis with iritis, iridocyclitis, vitritis, panuveitis, iris atrophy, iris crystalline deposits, and Fuchs heterochromic iridocyclitis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r58">[58]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Lens findings:&#x000a0;</bold>Cataract and lenticular dislocation</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Intraocular pressure alterations:</bold>&#x000a0;Elevated intraocular pressure with acute trabeculitis or end-stage neovascular glaucoma, and ocular hypotony with iridocyclitis, ciliary body hypotrophy, or atrophy</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Retinal findings:</bold> Retinal vasculitis, neuroretinitis, telangiectasia, pigment epithelial changes, retinal folds, exudative retinal detachment, Coats disease, central retinal artery occlusion, chorioretinal atrophy, and retinitis pigmentosa-like changes&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Involutional stage:</bold> Multiple vitreoretinal or glaucoma filtration surgeries predispose the globe to involutional changes</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neuro-ophthalmologic findings:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p><bold>Optic nerve involvement:&#x000a0;</bold>Optic neuropathy and papillitis&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Cranial nerve palsy:</bold> Oculomotor and trochlear nerve palsy with restrictive strabismus</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Anisocoria</bold>: Horner syndrome and tonic pupil due to autonomic dysfunction</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neurologic findings:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p><bold>Epilepsy: </bold>Focal or generalized seizures or status epilepticus</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Pain:</bold> Headache, trigeminal neuralgia, and facial pain</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Cerebrovascular accidents:</bold> Ischemic stroke, cerebral hemorrhage, microinfarcts, microhemorrhages, and cerebral atrophy</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Vascular malformations:</bold> Aneurysms, cavernoma, hypoplastic or stenotic vessels</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Movement disorders:</bold> Muscle spasms, synkinesia, pyramidal signs, dystonia, torticollis, and gait disturbance</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Speech abnormalities:</bold> Aphasia and dysarthria</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Extremity abnormalities:</bold>&#x000a0;Extremity weakness, pain, or atrophy in rare cases&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Neuropsychiatric manifestations:</bold> Cognitive disturbance, hallucinations, and psychiatric symptoms</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Maxillofacial findings:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p><bold>Bony changes:</bold> Hypoplasia of the maxilla, zygomatic, mandible, temporal, and, rarely, frontal bones</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Oral manifestations:</bold> Unilateral tongue, gingival, and hard palate atrophy, and abnormal development of the teeth</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cardiac findings</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Rheumatologic heart disease</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Endocrine</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hashimoto thyroiditis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Metabolic disorder</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical History</bold>
</p>
        <p>The onset of Parry-Romberg syndrome typically occurs during the first to second decade of life, although adult-onset cases have been reported. The disease usually begins insidiously and progresses over several years before stabilizing. Patients or their guardians often report gradual facial asymmetry, first noticed as subtle flattening or thinning on one side of the face. Atrophy usually begins in the maxillary or buccal region, progressing superiorly or inferiorly to involve the forehead, chin, and occasionally the neck (see <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Parry-Romberg Syndrome, Regional Progression). Patients may report facial discomfort, dysesthesia, or pain in the trigeminal nerve's dermatomal distribution, suggesting a neuropathic mechanism: localized hyperpigmentation or hypopigmentation precedes visible atrophy.</p>
        <p>A detailed timeline of disease progression is crucial. Early phases are often mistaken for localized scleroderma (<italic toggle="yes">en coup de sabre</italic>) or posttraumatic contour changes. Progression may occur in stages, including initial inflammation, active atrophy, and quiescence. Atrophy halts spontaneously after several years, though relapses may occur.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r59">[59]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients may experience blurred vision, diplopia, or enophthalmos&#x000a0;resulting from&#x000a0;orbital fat loss.&#x000a0;Dental malocclusion or delayed tooth eruption may occur in children due to asymmetric bone growth. Neurologic signs, including seizures, migraine-like headaches, trigeminal neuralgia, or focal weakness, are common in patients with cerebral involvement.</p>
        <p>Inquiring about associated autoimmune diseases is essential, as Parry-Romberg syndrome frequently overlaps with morphea, systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, or thyroid dysfunction. Some patients may recall antecedent trauma, infection (eg, herpes zoster, <italic toggle="yes">Borrelia </italic>spp), or surgery preceding onset, supporting theories of immune or inflammatory triggers. Family history is rarely positive, though a few familial clusters have been reported. Environmental exposures such as toxic chemicals, chronic stress, or viral illnesses may contribute. However, evidence supporting these associations remains circumstantial.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r60">[60]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Physical Examination</bold>
</p>
        <p>A meticulous facial examination under good lighting is essential. Inspection typically reveals unilateral, progressive atrophy involving the skin, subcutaneous tissue, muscles, and occasionally, the osseous structures of the face. The right and left sides should be compared carefully, noting loss of facial contour, decreased fullness, and visible asymmetry. The skin overlying the affected area appears thin, dry, and hyperpigmented or hypopigmented, with visible subcutaneous vessels. In some patients, an ivory-colored linear sclerotic band, reminiscent of linear morphea, may cross the forehead or scalp (termed <italic toggle="yes">en coup de sabre</italic>). Alopecia may occur in this region.</p>
        <p>Subcutaneous fat loss is the hallmark, resulting in deep nasolabial folds, hollowing of the cheek, and prominence of bony landmarks, such as the zygoma or mandible. In advanced disease, the muscles of facial expression,&#x000a0;especially the orbicularis oris, buccinator, and masseter, may be visibly wasted. This atrophy can result in&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">sunken eyes</italic>&#x000a0;(enophthalmos), deviation of the mouth toward the unaffected side, and facial asymmetry during&#x000a0;speech or mastication.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r61">[61]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Ocular findings</bold> include enophthalmos, upper eyelid retraction, ptosis, and loss of orbital fat. The cornea may show localized thinning or scarring. In patients with neuro-ophthalmic involvement, uveitis, keratitis, or optic neuritis may be present. Fundus examination may reveal vascular attenuation or peripapillary pigmentary changes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Oral examination</bold> may show hemiatrophy of the tongue, palate, or gingiva. The dental arch on the affected side may be narrow, with malalignment or delayed tooth eruption. Mandibular involvement can result in chin deviation and altered occlusion. In&#x000a0;pediatric patients with severe disease,&#x000a0;asymmetric growth of the facial bones leads to long-term craniofacial deformity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r62">[62]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Neurologic examination</bold> may reveal decreased sensation in one or more branches of the trigeminal nerve, hyperesthesia, or dysesthesia. In advanced stages, trigeminal neuralgia, facial paresthesia, and hemimigraine are typical. Some patients may exhibit seizure activity, typically focal and contralateral to the atrophic side, suggestive of ipsilateral cerebral involvement. MRI imaging findings may show white-matter hyperintensities or cortical atrophy corresponding to these symptoms.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Systemic examination</bold> should assess for associated conditions such as localized scleroderma, Raynaud phenomenon, or autoimmune thyroiditis. Cutaneous lesions elsewhere on the body may provide diagnostic clues to an underlying systemic connective tissue disorder.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r63">[63]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Laterality and Distribution</bold>
</p>
        <p>Parry-Romberg syndrome typically affects one&#x000a0;side of the face, with a left-sided predominance noted in approximately 60% to 70% of cases. Bilateral or alternating hemifacial involvement is exceedingly rare but has been described. The progression pattern usually follows a cranio-caudal gradient, beginning around the periorbital or malar region and extending inferiorly toward the mandible and neck. The involvement of extrafacial structures, such as the scalp, neck, trunk, and even the extremities, has been reported in atypical cases, suggesting that Parry-Romberg syndrome may represent a broader form of localized scleroderma with neurogenic components.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r59">[59]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Grading and Assessment</bold>
</p>
        <p>Although no universal staging system exists, clinicians often categorize Parry-Romberg syndrome severity into:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Mild:</bold> Soft-tissue atrophy confined to one facial region (eg, cheek or forehead)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Moderate:</bold> Atrophy involving multiple facial areas, including muscle loss and mild bone changes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Severe:</bold> Extensive involvement with skeletal deformity, ocular displacement, and dental malocclusion</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Photographic documentation</bold> is critical for longitudinal assessment and postoperative comparison.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r64">[64]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Differential Clinical Signs</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><italic toggle="yes"><bold>En coup de sabre&#x000a0;</bold></italic><bold>lesions</bold>&#x000a0;over the forehead may morphologically overlap with localized scleroderma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hypoplasia of the mandible or zygoma</bold> can mimic congenital craniofacial syndromes such as hemifacial microsomia.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cutaneous sclerosis</bold> may resemble morphea or posttraumatic scarring, but the gradual progression and neurologic features favor Parry-Romberg syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r65">[65]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Key Clinical Correlations</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neurologic signs,&#x000a0;</bold>including seizures, migraine, and neuralgic pain, suggest intracranial or neurovascular involvement, warranting an MRI brain with contrast.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ophthalmologic manifestations</bold>, especially enophthalmos and corneal changes, necessitate slit-lamp evaluation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Dental and skeletal abnormalities</bold> must be assessed using orthopantomography or facial CT.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Autoimmune markers</bold> (eg, ANA, anti-topoisomerase 1 [anti&#x02013;Scl-70], anti-dsDNA) may differentiate systemic sclerosis from other conditions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r66">[66]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 6. Summary of Clinical Findings</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>System involved</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Common findings</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Clinical implications</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Dermatologic</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hyperpigmentation, hypopigmentation, localized scleroderma</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early clue to autoimmune overlap</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Subcutaneous tissue</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Fat atrophy, vessel prominence</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Facial asymmetry, cosmetic deformity</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Muscular</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Weakness, wasting</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Functional deficits in mastication</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Skeletal</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Maxillary and mandibular hypoplasia</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Occlusal deformity, jaw asymmetry</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35.9092px;">
                <td style="height: 35.9092px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Ophthalmic</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35.9092px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Enophthalmos, keratitis, uveitis</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35.9092px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Visual impairment</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Neurologic</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Neuralgia, seizures, hemimigraine</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Central nervous system involvement</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Dental</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Malocclusion, delayed eruption</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Pediatric deformity</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Systemic</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Autoimmune overlap, Raynaud phenomenon</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Multisystemic implications</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s9" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Parry-Romberg syndrome is primarily a clinical diagnosis. Various modalities can be employed to&#x000a0;exclude any similarly presenting syndromes (see&#x000a0;<bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Parry-Romberg Syndrome Evaluation&#x000a0;Algorithm). A summary of the evaluation modalities is provided in <bold>Table 7.</bold></p>
        <p>
<bold>Ocular Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Best-corrected visual acuity, refraction, and pupillary reaction assessment</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>General examination for symmetry of the face, head posture, extraocular muscle movements, and strabismus</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Orbital examination, evaluation of eyelid abnormalities, and ptosis evaluation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Slit-lamp examination</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intraocular pressure measurement by Goldmann applanation tonometry</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Detailed fundus examination</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Orbital ultrasonography: In patients with media opacity due to vitritis, cataract, or corneal opacity, this modality aids in diagnosing posterior segment pathology. Ultrasonography biomicroscopy is used to detect changes in the ciliary body&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r20">[20]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Orbital imaging: Orbital CT is used to evaluate bony changes within the orbit.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Neurology Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Electroencephalogram (EEG):&#x000a0;</bold>For the localization of a cerebral lesion in epilepsy&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cerebrospinal fluid analysis:</bold>&#x000a0;Cerebrospinal fluid examination findings are usually normal, but oligoclonal bands and a raised IgG index are associated with T2-weighted hyperintense lesions on MRI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r24">[24]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neuroimaging:</bold>&#x000a0;A CT scan delineates the cranial bony deformity, thinning of the diplo&#x000eb;, and intracranial calcifications. MRI T1-weighted sequences localize patches of cerebral atrophy. MRI T2-weighted sequence identifies the hyperintense foci in the subcortical region. Magnetic resonance angiogram findings demonstrate the extent and location of vascular malformations. Brain spectral photoemission CT scan can detect subclinical CNS lesions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r67">[67]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Dental Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Dental evaluation monitors progressive deformity of the mandible and abnormalities of the teeth by serial orthopantomograms and photographs</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Dermatology Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Skin biopsy is not performed routinely. However, the histopathologic findings help differentiate Parry-Romberg syndrome from linear scleroderma&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">en coup de sabre</italic>.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The evaluation of Parry-Romberg Syndrome<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>requires an interprofessional approach that combines clinical observation with advanced laboratory, imaging, and histopathologic techniques. Because Parry-Romberg syndrome presents as a heterogeneous and multisystemic disorder, diagnostic evaluation aims to confirm the clinical diagnosis, identify comorbid autoimmune or neurologic components, and quantify the extent of tissue and structural involvement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r60">[60]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Laboratory Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Although no single laboratory test definitively confirms Parry-Romberg syndrome, several serologic and immunologic markers assist in diagnosis and distinguishing autoimmune variants.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Autoimmune Serology</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>ANA:</bold> Positive result in up to 25% to 52% of patients, indicating autoimmune dysregulation&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r68">[68]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Anti-dsDNA</bold>&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;<bold>anti&#x02013;Scl-70</bold>: Occasionally detected, especially in overlap syndromes with localized scleroderma (<italic toggle="yes">en coup de sabre</italic>)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Anticentromere</bold> and <bold>antihistone antibodies:</bold> Rare but can occur in systemic variants</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Rheumatoid factor</bold>&#x000a0;and <bold>anti&#x02013;cyclic citrulinated peptide</bold><bold>:</bold> Useful when rheumatoid arthritis overlap is suspected;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r69">[69]</xref>&#x000a0;Elevated titers in patients with localized scleroderma and linear scleroderma&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">en coup de sabre</italic>&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r70">[70]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Other serologic tests:&#x000a0;</bold>C-reactive protein, <italic toggle="yes">HLA-B27</italic> typing, extractable nuclear antigen, and anticardiolipin antibodies are of limited value.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Inflammatory and Metabolic Parameters</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Erythrocyte sedimentation rate</bold>&#x000a0;<bold>and</bold>
<bold>C-reactive protein levels</bold>&#x000a0;may show mild elevation during the active inflammatory phase. See StatPearls' companion resource, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/21307">"Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate,"</ext-link> for further information.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Serum complement levels (C3, C4)</bold> can indicate immune complex activation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>A thyroid profile</bold>&#x000a0;(triiodothyronine, thyroxine, thyroid-stimulating hormone, and antithyroid antibodies) is recommended, given the frequent coexistence of autoimmune thyroiditis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Serum vitamin D</bold>
<bold>and autoantibody panels</bold> (eg, antiphospholipid, antineuronal) may be included in the evaluation of refractory or multisystemic disease.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Infectious Disease Screening</bold>
</p>
        <p>Given proposed links to <italic toggle="yes">Borrelia burgdorferi</italic>, herpes simplex virus, and varicella zoster virus, serological tests for these pathogens may be warranted. Polymerase chain reaction assays on skin biopsy tissue may confirm latent infection.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Radiologic Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)</bold>
</p>
        <p>MRI is the cornerstone of diagnostic imaging in Parry-Romberg syndrome, especially when neurologic symptoms such as seizures, migraine, or focal deficits are present. The imaging protocol should include T1-weighted, T2-weighted, and fluid-attenuated inversion recovery (FLAIR)-weighted sequences with gadolinium enhancement to detect:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>White-matter hyperintensities:</bold>&#x000a0;Observed in 60% to 80% of patients, often ipsilateral to the facial atrophy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cortical or subcortical atrophy:</bold> Suggesting chronic ischemic or inflammatory insult</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Leptomeningeal enhancement:</bold> Indicates a vasculitic or inflammatory process</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Calcifications:</bold> Detected in the basal ganglia or periventricular areas in chronic stages</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Trigeminal nerve involvement:</bold> May show thickening or enhancement along the ophthalmic or maxillary branches</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Advanced imaging techniques, including&#x000a0;diffusion tensor imaging and&#x000a0;magnetic resonance spectroscopy,&#x000a0;can help assess microstructural integrity and metabolic alterations in the affected hemisphere.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r71">[71]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Computed tomography (CT)</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>3D CT facial bone reconstruction:</bold>&#x000a0;Delineates the degree of skeletal asymmetry, mandibular hypoplasia, and zygomatic bone involvement. CT is helpful for presurgical planning in reconstructive procedures</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Craniofacial CT: </bold>May reveal ipsilateral thinning of the maxillary sinus wall and reduced volume of facial bones</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Ultrasonography and Doppler studies</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>High-resolution B-scan ultrasonography of the face:</bold>&#x000a0;Assesses subcutaneous and muscular atrophy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Color Doppler imaging:</bold>&#x000a0;Used to detect vascular compromise and decreased perfusion, which supports the neurovasculitis hypothesis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r72">[72]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Ocular imaging</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Anterior segment optical coherence tomography (AS-OCT):</bold> Reveals corneal thinning, irregular epithelium, and altered tear film profile</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Orbital MRI/CT:</bold> Evaluates orbital fat atrophy, extraocular muscle thinning, and enophthalmos severity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Fundus fluorescein angiography</bold>&#x000a0;<bold>and</bold>
<bold>optical coherence tomography (OCT):</bold> Identifies retinal vascular attenuation and optic nerve changes</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Neurological and Neurophysiological Assessment</bold>
</p>
        <p>Parry-Romberg syndrome frequently exhibits neurologic manifestations. A detailed neurological evaluation is essential, particularly with overlap syndromes (localized scleroderma or Rasmussen encephalitis).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r73">[73]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Electroencephalography (EEG)</bold>
</p>
        <p>EEG often shows focal epileptiform discharges or slow-wave abnormalities contralateral to the atrophic side, correlating with MRI lesions. Serial EEG monitoring facilitates seizure management and&#x000a0;tracks seizure progression.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Electromyography (EMG) and nerve conduction studies</bold>
</p>
        <p>These studies detect denervation and myopathic changes in facial muscles, supporting the trophoneurosis hypothesis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r74">[74]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Neuropsychological Assessment</bold>
</p>
        <p>Chronic Parry-Romberg syndrome can be associated with cognitive deficits, anxiety, or body-image disturbance. Neuropsychological&#x000a0;testing is recommended in patients undergoing reconstructive therapy or long-term immunosuppression.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Dermatologic and Histopathologic Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Skin biopsy</bold> from the atrophic area, when performed, reveals:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Epidermal atrophy with loss of adnexal structures</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Collagen homogenization and hyalinization of the dermis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Perivascular lymphocytic infiltration, suggestive of vasculitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Occasional sclerosis resembling morphea-type lesions</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Direct immunofluorescence</bold> can demonstrate immune-complex deposition along vascular and dermal&#x02013;epidermal junctions, reinforcing autoimmune pathogenesis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r75">[75]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Dental, Orthodontic, and Maxillofacial Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Panoramic radiography</bold><bold>&#x000a0;</bold>helps detect alveolar bone resorption, dental malocclusion, and delayed tooth eruption.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cephalometric analysis</bold> quantifies maxillomandibular discrepancies.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>3D stereophotogrammetry </bold>(<bold>facial surface scanning)</bold>&#x000a0;aids in the preoperative and postoperative documentation of asymmetry and reconstructive outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r76">[76]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Systemic and Autoimmune Screening</bold>
</p>
        <p>In line with international recommendations (eg, European Reference Network for Rare Skin Diseases, 2022), comprehensive systemic screening is essential:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cardiac evaluation:</bold> Echocardiography to rule out associated congenital vascular anomalies</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Renal and hepatic panels:</bold> Before systemic immunomodulatory therapy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Autoimmune profile:</bold>&#x000a0;ANA, extractable nuclear antigen, anti-dsDNA, rheumatoid factor, antineutrophil cytoplasm antibody, and antithyroid antibodies</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Lipid and metabolic profile:</bold> Especially when corticosteroids or methotrexate are considered&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r77">[77]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>International and National Guidelines</bold>
</p>
        <p>While no specific guidelines exist exclusively for Parry-Romberg syndrome, evaluation follows protocols adapted from related autoimmune and neurocutaneous syndromes, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>European League Against Rheumatism </bold>guidelines for morphea and localized scleroderma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>American Academy of Neurology</bold>&#x000a0;recommendations for neuroimaging in vasculitic disorders</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Indian Council of Medical Research</bold>&#x000a0;guidelines on the evaluation of rare diseases and interprofessional referral protocols</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>&#x000a0;Disease Activity Monitoring</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Serial photography</bold>&#x000a0;every 6 to 12 months to document disease progression or stabilization.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>MRI surveillance</bold>&#x000a0;at 12- to 24-month intervals helps detect cerebral or vascular changes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Patient-reported outcome measures and facial symmetry indices</bold> quantify the psychological and cosmetic impact.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r78">[78]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>&#x000a0;Table 7. </bold>Diagnostic Evaluation in Parry-Romberg Syndrome</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table6" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Category</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Diagnostic test</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Key findings</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Clinical utility</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Immunology</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>ANA, anti-dsDNA, anti&#x02013;Scl-70</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Positive in autoimmune overlap</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Confirms immune etiology</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Imaging</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>MRI brain, CT face</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>White matter lesions, bone asymmetry</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Quantifies neurologic and facial involvement</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48.2229px;">
                <td style="height: 48.2229px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Neurology</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48.2229px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>EEG, EMG</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48.2229px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Epileptiform discharges, muscle denervation</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48.2229px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Correlates with the neurotrophic hypothesis</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Ophthalmic</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>AS-OCT, orbital MRI</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Enophthalmos, corneal thinning</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Monitors ocular complications</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Histopathology</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Skin biopsy</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Dermal sclerosis, vasculitis</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Supports autoimmune pathogenesis</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Dental</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Panoramic radiography, cephalometry</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Mandibular hypoplasia</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Orthodontic planning</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Systemic</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Autoimmune, liver, and renal function panels</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Associated disorders</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Multisystem screening</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Abbreviations:</italic> ANA, antinuclear antibody; anti-dsDNA, anti&#x02013;double-stranded DNA; anti&#x02013;Scl-70, anti-topoisomerase I; AS-OCT, anterior segment optical coherence tomography; EEG, electroencephalogram; EMG, electromyography.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s10" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Goals of Care</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Control active disease</bold> (inflammation, vasculitis, neuroinflammation) to halt progression</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Protect the&#x000a0;</bold>ocular surface, mastication, dentition, speech, and neurological status</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Rehabilitate form</bold> (soft tissue and skeletal volume/symmetry) once the disease is quiescent</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Support mental health</bold> (body-image, anxiety, depression) and quality of life&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r60">[60]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r79">[79]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>A summary of the step-wise approach to treatment is presented in <bold>Table 8.</bold></p>
        <p>
<bold>Step 1: Determine Disease Activity&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Suggestive of activity</bold>: New or enlarging areas of atrophy or dyspigmentation over months; neuropathic facial pain; worsening enophthalmos; new seizures or MRI enhancement (white-matter lesions, leptomeningeal enhancement); elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate/C-reactive protein levels; dermal warmth or tenderness</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Quiescence</bold>: &#x02265;12 to 24 months without change on serial photographs or 3D surface scans, and stable imaging</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Treat active Parry-Romberg syndrome like localized scleroderma with neurovascularitic features; defer definitive reconstruction until the condition is&#x000a0;quiescent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r60">[60]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Step 2: Medical Therapy for Active Parry-Romberg Syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>(Adapted from EULAR recommendations for localized scleroderma and neuroinflammatory overlap.)</p>
        <p>
<bold>First-line disease control&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Methotrexate:</bold> 15-25 mg/wk orally or subcutaneously + folic acid 1-5 mg/d (omit on methotrexate day)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Pediatric: 0.3&#x02013;0.6 mg/kg/wk (maximum 25 mg)</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Onset 6-12 weeks; continue 12-24 months, then taper over 3-6 months if stable</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Monitor complete blood count, aspartate transaminase, alanine transaminase, and serum creatinine levels every 4-8 weeks; avoid use in pregnancy; vaccinate according to guidance</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Systemic corticosteroid:</bold>&#x000a0;Bridge for 6-12 weeks in active disease or neuro-ocular involvement</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p><bold>Prednisone:</bold> 0.5-1 mg/kg/d with taper over 6-8 weeks <bold>or</bold></p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p><bold>Intravenous methylprednisolone:</bold>&#x000a0;500-1000 mg/d for 3 consecutive days for rapidly progressive lesions or neuroinflammation&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r80">[80]</xref></p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Second-line and steroid-sparing options</bold> (use when methotrexate is contraindicated or inadequate response after approximately 3-6 months)</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Mycophenolate mofetil:</bold> 1-1.5 g twice daily&#x000a0;(children, 600 mg/m&#x000b2; twice daily)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Azathioprine:</bold> 1.5-2 mg/kg/d (check thiopurine methyltransferase activity before initiation)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cyclosporine:</bold> 3-4 mg/kg/d (refractory disease; monitor blood pressure and renal function)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hydroxychloroquine:</bold> 200-400 mg/d as adjunct (ophthalmic screening every 6-12 months)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r81">[81]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Biologic or targeted therapy (refractory disease)</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Rituximab:</bold>&#x000a0;B-cell depletion; 1000 mg intravenous (IV) on day 1 and 15; repeat in approximately 6-12 months if relapse occurs</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Tocilizumab:</bold>&#x000a0;IL-6 inhibitor; 8 mg/kg IV monthly or 162 mg subcutaneously weekly</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Infliximab/adalimumab</bold>: May be helpful when morphea overlaps</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Adjuncts</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ultraviolet A1 phototherapy</bold> (morphea-type inflammatory plaques): 30-60 J/cm&#x000b2;, 3-5 times per week for 6-10 weeks (where available)<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r82">[82]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neuropathic pain</bold>: Gabapentin, pregabalin, tricyclic antidepressants, serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors; short corticosteroid burst if inflammatory pain</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Seizure management</bold>: Standard antiseizure medications (levetiracetam preferred); treat concomitant neuroinflammation as above</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ocular surface protection</bold>: Lubricants, moisture goggles, punctal plugs; treat keratitis and uveitis per ophthalmology recommendations&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r59">[59]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Monitoring</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Baseline and every 3-6 months:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Serial photography</p></list-item><list-item><p>3D imaging</p></list-item><list-item><p>Patient-reported outcome measures (body image, quality of life)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Neurologic examination</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ophthalmic slit-lamp examination</p></list-item><list-item><p>MRI if symptomatic</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Medication&#x000a0;monitoring and contraception counseling are required for teratogens (methotrexate, mycophenolate mofetil). <bold>Table 9 </bold>lists the monitoring requirements for the use of immunosuppressants.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Step 3: Reconstructive and Functional Surgery (Quiescent Phase)</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Timing</bold>: Ideally, after &#x02265;12&#x02013;24 months of inactivity (documented clinically by photography and 3D imaging with a stable MRI). Earlier targeted procedures are reasonable for vision-threatening enophthalmos, exposure keratopathy, or severe psychosocial distress.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Soft-tissue augmentation</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<bold>Autologous fat grafting (Coleman microfat/structural fat technique)</bold>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Pros: Natural feel, repeatable, low donor morbidity; can begin once activity slows; useful for staged contour restoration</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Cons: 30% to 50% resorption,<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>plan overcorrection, and staged sessions 3-6 months apart</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p><bold>Nanofat/stromal vascular fraction&#x02013;enriched fat</bold> may enhance skin quality, but evidence is still emerging</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Dermal-fat grafts</bold>
<bold>(iliac crest/abdomen):</bold> Greater structural volume, single larger correction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Acellular dermal matrix or fascia lata</bold>: Used to camouflage contour defects or support eyelids</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Alloplastic implants</bold>&#x000a0;<bold>(porous polyethylene, silicone)</bold> are considered for a stable bone framework and are helpful for malar or mandibular contour; infection/extrusion risk is slightly higher in thin skin&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r83">[83]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Free-flap options (moderate&#x02013;severe atrophy)</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Anterolateral thigh, parascapular, deep inferior epigastric perforator, or latissimus dorsi free flaps provide robust, vascularized volume with durable symmetry; often combined with secondary debulking or lipofilling</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Skeletal correction and occlusion</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Orthognathic surgery</bold> (Le Fort, sagittal split osteotomy) for maxillo-mandibular discrepancy and malocclusion; often staged after soft-tissue volume is restored</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Genioplasty/implants</bold> for chin deviation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Distraction osteogenesis</bold> in pediatric growth disturbance (specialized centers)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r84">[84]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Ocular and periocular procedures</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Orbital volume augmentation: </bold>Fat grafts or alloplastic implants for enophthalmos</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Eyelid retraction repair/ptosis correction:</bold>&#x000a0;Canthoplasty; address exposure keratopathy first</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Strabismus</bold>&#x000a0;<bold>surgery:</bold>&#x000a0;For extraocular imbalance</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Dental/orthodontic</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Early orthodontic planning</bold>: Retainers, aligners, or staged orthognathic correction once facial growth stabilizes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Prosthodontic</bold>&#x000a0;<bold>solutions:</bold> For occlusal plane discrepancies&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r85">[85]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Step 4: Rehabilitation and Supportive Care</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Physical therapy:</bold>&#x000a0;For temporomandibular function, scar pliability, and facial symmetry training</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Camouflage dermatology:</bold>&#x000a0;For dyspigmentation, sun protection, and treatment of alopecia in <italic toggle="yes">en coup de sabre</italic></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Psychological support:</bold>&#x000a0;Cognitive behavioral therapy, support groups; screen adolescents for anxiety, depression, and bullying</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Social/educational accommodations:</bold>&#x000a0;For patients requiring recurrent medical or surgical care</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Special Situations</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Children/adolescents</bold>: Favor early medical disease control; staged fat grafting can mitigate the psychosocial burden before definitive skeletal surgery</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Pregnancy</bold>: Avoid methotrexate, mycophenolate mofetil, cyclophosphamide; consider azathioprine or low-dose prednisone; consult with high-risk obstetrics</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Perioperative immunosuppression</bold>: Time elective flaps or implants when corticosteroid dose minimized; ensure infection prophylaxis and venous thromboembolism prevention</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Relapse after reconstruction</bold>: Return to step 2; anticipate revision lipofilling&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r86">[86]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>Ophthalmic Management</bold></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Acute uveitis:&#x000a0;</bold>Topical cycloplegics and topical and systemic corticosteroids are the mainstay treatments. Acute trabeculitis is treated with topical antiglaucoma medications. Relapses are common, and aggressive management is often required.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Keratitis:</bold>&#x000a0;Eyelid taping and profuse topical lubrication are helpful for exposure keratopathy. Early diagnosis and treatment of infective keratitis is imperative.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Corneal opacity:</bold>&#x000a0;An optical penetrating keratoplasty may restore vision for severely opacified corneas resulting from exposure keratopathy, infectious keratitis, and primary endothelial failure.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Scleral melt:</bold> Scleral patch graft is the preferred treatment.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Glaucoma:&#x000a0;</bold>Antiglaucoma medications can control the intraocular pressure in early disease. Severe disease with inadequate control requires trabeculectomy surgery.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Restrictive strabismus:</bold>&#x000a0;The small-angle deviations are corrected by prism glasses. Large-angle strabismus often requires definitive surgical correction.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Retinal vasculitis:</bold>&#x000a0;Active disease shows vascular lesions in the mid-peripheral retina and mild vitritis. Topical and systemic immunosuppression is the primary treatment. Advanced disease shows neovascularization, vitreous hemorrhage, or tractional retinal detachment. The visible nonperfused retina is lasered in patients with posterior segment neovascularization. Vitreoretinal surgery with intravitreal injection of anti&#x02013;vascular endothelial growth factor is used for nonresolving vitreous hemorrhage and tractional retinal detachment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r87">[87]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Neurological Management</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Seizures: </bold>Antiepileptic drugs are titrated for long-term control.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neuropsychiatric disorders:</bold>&#x000a0;Psychiatric consultation is essential.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Rehabilitation Strategies</bold>
</p>
        <p>Cosmetic rehabilitation is attempted after 1 to&#x000a0;2 years of&#x000a0;quiescence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r88">[88]</xref>&#x000a0;Common interventions include:</p>
        <p>
<bold>Oculoplastic management</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Enophthalmos:&#x000a0;</bold>Intraorbital injection of fillers, such as silicone gel, hyaluronate derivatives, or autologous fat grafts with adipose-derived stem cells, yields good cosmetic results.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ptosis:&#x000a0;</bold>Primary&#x000a0;correction of the enophthalmos often provides satisfactory cosmesis. Frontalis sling surgery is reserved for severe disease.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Eyelid retraction:</bold> Surgically treated with levator recession or levator lengthening with the relaxing myotomy technique.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Lagophthalmos:</bold>&#x000a0;Surgical correction with permanent lateral tarsorrhaphy is preferred.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Drooping of eyebrow:</bold>&#x000a0;Browpexy or Z-plasty are helpful treatment options.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Atrophic/phthisis bulbi:</bold>&#x000a0;Painless involutional globe with adequately formed conjunctival fornices requires a primary trial of the prosthetic shell. The painful phthisical eye may require enucleation surgery with a silicone implant.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Contracted socket:</bold>&#x000a0;Mucous membrane and fat grafting are needed for total surgical socket reconstruction in severe disease with forniceal shelving before prosthetic shell trial.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r89">[89]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Plastic surgery management</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Synthetic tissue fillers: </bold>A&#x000a0;minimally invasive modality to correct facial asymmetry with silicone gel, polyethylene, hydroxyapatite, and poly-L-lactate that is beneficial for mild to moderate disease&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r90">[90]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Autologous fat grafting:&#x000a0;</bold>The Coleman technique&#x000a0;has acceptable cosmetic outcomes&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r91">[91]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Skeletal reconstruction:</bold>&#x000a0;Bone paste cranioplasty, bone implants, bone/cartilage grafts, and orthognathic surgery have variable success rates</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Free soft-tissue transplant:</bold>&#x000a0;Used for large-volume reconstruction. Commonly used flap sites are the greater omentum, lateral thigh, parascapular muscles, latissimus dorsi muscle, and the dorsal thoracic adipofascial flap. This procedure provides satisfactory cosmetic outcomes&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table7" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 8. Phase-Based Management</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Disease phase</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Primary goals</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Typical interventions</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Active/ progressive</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Halt progression; treat pain/seizures; protect the eye</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Methotrexate &#x000b1; corticosteroid bridge; mycophenolate/azathioprine if needed; biologic rescue; ocular surface care; neurology comanagement</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Early stabilization</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Document quiescence; plan rehabilitation</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Serial photos/3D scans; trial lipofilling for contour; psychosocial support</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Quiescent (&#x02265;12-24 mo)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Restore symmetry and function</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Staged fat grafting; dermal-fat/acellular dermal matrices; free flaps for volume; orthognathic surgery; orbital/eyelid procedures; dental alignment</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Long-term</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Maintain results; monitor relapse</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Annual review; MRI if neurologic symptoms; minor revision lipofilling</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table8" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 9. Common Medications and Monitoring Requirements</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 124px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Drug</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 152px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Dose (adult)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 140.955px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Key monitoring</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 217.045px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Notes</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 124px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Methotrexate</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 152px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>15&#x02013;25 mg/wk with folate</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 140.955px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>CBC, LFTs, Cr every 4-8 weeks</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 217.045px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>12&#x02013;24 months then taper; teratogenic</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 124px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Prednisone</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 152px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>0.5&#x02013;1 mg/kg/d, taper over 6&#x02013;8 weeks</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 140.955px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Blood pressure, glucose, and osteoporosis</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 217.045px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Consider intravenous methylprednisolone pulses for rapid control</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 124px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Mycophenolate</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 152px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>1&#x02013;1.5 g twice daily</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 140.955px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>CBC, LFTs, pregnancy test</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 217.045px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Gastrointestinal tract discomfort; teratogenic</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 124px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Azathioprine</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 152px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>1.5&#x02013;2 mg/kg/d</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 140.955px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>CBC, LFTs; TPMT</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 217.045px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Avoid with low TPMT</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 124px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Rituximab</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 152px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>1000 mg on day 1 and 15</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 140.955px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>CD19, IgG; infection</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 217.045px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Vaccinate before therapy</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 124px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Tocilizumab</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 152px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>162 mg/wk SC weekly or 8 mg/kg IV every 4 weeks</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 140.955px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>LFTs, lipids, CBC</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 217.045px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Monitor for infection and gastrointestinal tract perforation risk</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 124px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Hydroxychloroquine</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 152px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>200&#x02013;400 mg/d</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 140.955px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Baseline &#x00026; annual eye exam</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 217.045px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Skin/joint symptoms adjunct</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Abbreviations:</italic> CBC, complete blood cell count; Cr, serum creatinine; IgG, immunoglobulin G; IV, intravenous; LFTs, liver function tests; SC, subcutaneous; TPMT, thiopurine methyltransferase;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Interprofessional Coordination&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Dermatology/rheumatology</bold>: Disease activity, immunomodulation, phototherapy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neurology</bold>: Seizures, neuroinflammation, MRI/EEG surveillance</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ophthalmology</bold>: Ocular surface, uveitis, enophthalmos</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Craniofacial plastics/ear, nose, and throat specialists/orofacial myofunctional disorder specialists</bold>: Soft-tissue and skeletal reconstruction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Orthodontics/prosthodontics</bold>: Occlusion and dentoalveolar rehabilitation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Psychology/psychiatry:</bold>&#x000a0;Coping, body-image, adherence</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Rehabilitation and nursing</bold>: Scar care, postoperative support, patient education</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Key Takeaways</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Treat inflammation first; reconstruct after quiescence.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Methotrexate with a short course of corticosteroids&#x000a0;is the first-line regimen;&#x000a0;mycophenolate mofetil and methotrexate, as well as biologics, are used for refractory disease.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Staged autologous fat grafting is used for contour restoration; free flaps for severe atrophy; and orthognathic surgery for skeletal asymmetry.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Continuous psychosocial and functional support is as important as surgical correction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r92">[92]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s11" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis for Parry-Romberg syndrome includes the following:</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Rasmussen encephalitis:</bold>&#x000a0;This condition presents with unilateral cerebral involvement and intractable seizures. Focal neurological deficits and subsequent atrophy mimic the presentation of Parry-Romberg syndrome. Histopathologic findings of lymphocytic vasculitis in both Rasmussen encephalitis and Parry-Romberg syndrome may complicate diagnosis. Parry-Romberg syndrome may be associated with Rasmussen encephalitis, leading to progressive hemifacial atrophy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r93">[93]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Barraquer-Simons syndrome:</bold>&#x000a0;An acquired, progressive lipodystrophy disorder that affects the face, neck, thorax, abdomen, and, rarely,&#x000a0;the extremities. Central nervous system involvement is suggested by epilepsy and bilateral cerebral atrophy.&#x000a0;A gradual, symmetric, bilateral loss of subcutaneous fat tissue, with renal involvement, helps differentiate this syndrome from Parry-Romberg syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r94">[94]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hemifacial hypertrophy:</bold>&#x000a0;This condition involves the asymmetric enlargement of one half of the face and head, rather than hemiatrophy, resulting in an asymmetric facial deformity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r95">[95]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hemifacial fat necrosis:</bold> The causes range from bulbar poliomyelitis and connective tissue diseases to trauma. The absence of muscular or osseous involvement helps differentiate these causes from Parry-Romberg syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r96">[96]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Congenital deformities:</bold> Conditions such as congenital hemiatrophy or torticollis may mimic facial asymmetry seen in Parry-Romberg syndrome. A careful history of onset helps differentiate these entities.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Table 10&#x000a0;</bold>lists the differential diagnoses and the key differentiating factors.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table9" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 10. Differential Diagnosis of Parry&#x02013;Romberg Syndrome</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Condition</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Distinguishing features</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Investigations/key findings</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Comparison with Parry-Romberg syndrome</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Linear scleroderma (<italic toggle="yes">en coup de sabre</italic>)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Linear, depressed sclerotic band usually on the forehead/scalp; may cross midline; associated with alopecia and skin tightening</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Skin biopsy shows dense collagen deposition and perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate.</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>In Parry-Romberg syndrome, atrophy is more diffuse and involves soft tissues, bone, and muscle without a sclerotic band</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Hemifacial microsomia (oculo-auriculo-vertebral spectrum)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Congenital ear anomalies (microtia, preauricular tags), mandibular hypoplasia, epibulbar dermoids</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>CT: Hypoplastic mandible, absent ear canal; no inflammatory features</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Parry&#x02013;Romberg syndrome is acquired, progressive, and not associated with ear anomalies or vertebral defects</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Localized lipodystrophy (partial lipodystrophy, Barraquer&#x02013;Simons)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Symmetrical or regional loss of subcutaneous fat, often starting in the face and spreading to the trunk</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Low C3 levels are associated with renal disease (membranoproliferative GN)</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Parry-Romberg syndrome is unilateral, associated with neurologic or autoimmune findings, and involves muscle/bone</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Rasmussen encephalitis</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Progressive focal seizures, hemiparesis, and atrophy of one cerebral hemisphere</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>MRI: Unilateral cortical atrophy with T2-weighted hyperintensity</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Neurological overlap possible, but Parry-Romberg syndrome includes facial atrophy and dermatologic features absent in Rasmussen</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Progressive hemifacial lipomatosis</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Unilateral overgrowth rather than atrophy; facial asymmetry with hypertrophy of fat&#x000a0;</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>MRI: Increased subcutaneous fat; no loss of bony or muscular mass</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>The opposite of Parry-Romberg syndrome, characterized by hypertrophy instead of atrophy</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Posttraumatic fat necrosis/atrophy</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>History of trauma or surgery; localized depression at the injury site</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>History and imaging are consistent with focal fat loss</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Parry-Romberg syndrome progresses without antecedent trauma in most cases</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy&#x000a0;</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Autosomal dominant; facial weakness, scapular winging, muscle wasting</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>EMG: Myopathic changes; genetic testing (<italic toggle="yes">D4Z4</italic> contraction on chromosome 4q35)</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Muscle weakness is diffuse and bilateral; Parry-Romberg syndrome affects soft tissue and bone unilaterally</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Chronic cleroderma/systemic ssclerosis</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Widespread skin thickening, Raynaud phenomenon, and internal organ involvement</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Positive ANA, anti&#x02013;Scl-70 titers</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Parry-Romberg syndrome is limited to the face, lacks Raynaud and systemic fibrosis</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Hemifacial spasm with atrophy</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Involuntary twitching or spasms may lead to mild atrophy from disuse</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>EMG, abnormal findings; normal MRI findings</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Parry-Romberg syndrome shows progressive structural loss rather than functional muscle atrophy</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Hansen disease (leprosy)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hypopigmented anesthetic patches and thickened nerves may cause facial atrophy</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Skin biopsy: Acid-fast bacilli; lepromin test</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Sensory loss and nerve thickening predominate; not autoimmune or neurovasculitic</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Craniofacial vascular malformations (AVM/hemangioma)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Facial asymmetry due to vascular proliferation, warmth, bruit, pulsation</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Doppler, MR angiography</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Parry-Romberg syndrome shows progressive atrophy without vascular proliferation or bruit</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Localized myositis/myopathy</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Painful or inflammatory muscle loss</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>CK level elevated; biopsy: inflammation or necrosis</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Parry-Romberg syndrome has no elevation of CK levels and involves fat, bone, and skin beyond muscle</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Congenital facial nerve palsy (Moebius syndrome)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Present at birth; bilateral facial weakness, gaze palsy</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>MRI: Brainstem hypoplasia</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Parry-Romberg syndrome is acquired and progressive; Moebius is static and congenital</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Autoimmune polyglandular syndrome with lipoatrophy</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Endocrine abnormalities (Addison disease, thyroid, diabetes mellitus); lipodystrophy</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Endocrine assays; autoantibodies</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Parry-Romberg syndrome lacks multiendocrine involvement</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Infectious granulomatous diseases (eg, tuberculosis, sarcoidosis)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Indurated nodules or plaques; multisystem involvement</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Biopsy: granulomatous inflammation; chest x-ray/ACE level</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Parry-Romberg syndrome shows smooth skin atrophy without granulomas</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Abbreviations:</italic> ACE, angiotensin-converting enzyme; ANA, antinuclear antibody; AVM, arteriovenous malformation; CK, creatine kinase; EMG, electromyography; GN, glomerulonephritis.</p>
        <p>Differentiating Parry-Romberg Syndrome from other causes of facial asymmetry requires a combination of:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Clinical history:</bold> Progressive, unilateral, acquired atrophy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Imaging:</bold>&#x000a0;MRI with soft-tissue and bone changes without a mass lesion</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Autoimmune markers:</bold>&#x000a0;ANA, anti&#x02013;Scl-70 to evaluate for overlap with scleroderma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Histopathology:</bold>&#x000a0;Atrophy without fibrosis, typical of morphea</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s12" sec-type="Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials">
        <title>Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials</title>
        <p>Despite being a rare and heterogeneous disorder, several clinical and translational studies have contributed to understanding the pathogenesis, disease course, and therapeutic responses in Parry-Romberg Syndrome (see<bold> Image.</bold> Pathophysiology-Targeted Treatments for Parry-Romberg syndrome). Although randomized controlled trials are limited by the low incidence of the disease, observational studies, case series, and pilot trials of immunosuppressive and reconstructive modalities have provided valuable evidence for clinical management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r47">[47]</xref>&#x000a0;The key clinical trials are summarized in <bold>Table 11.</bold></p>
        <p>
<bold>1. Immunomodulatory Therapy and Disease Control</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold> Methotrexate and corticosteroid therapy</bold> Shah et al published one of the most extensive multicenter retrospective studies involving 94 patients with localized scleroderma and Parry-Romberg syndrome overlap, evaluating systemic methotrexate (15-25 mg/wk) combined with corticosteroids (prednisone 0.5-1 mg/kg/d). The study findings demonstrated significant clinical remission in 84% of patients within 6 to 9 months, with stabilization of facial atrophy and cutaneous sclerosis.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Outcome:</bold> Disease arrest achieved in most patients within&#x000a0;1 year; relapse rate &#x0003c;20% over 3 years</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Relevance:</bold> Validates methotrexate as the first-line systemic immunomodulator in Parry-Romberg syndrome with inflammatory activity&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r97">[97]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Mycophenolate mofetil and biologics in refractory Parry-Romberg syndrome</bold> Arif et al&#x000a0;evaluated 27 patients with refractory Parry-Romberg syndrome/linear morphea who failed methotrexate therapy. Mycophenolate mofetil (1-1.5 g twice daily) achieved disease stabilization in 70% of patients within 6 months. Case reports using biologics such as rituximab and tocilizumab have shown promising results, particularly in patients with neurovasculitic manifestations or refractory progression.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Outcome:</bold> Rituximab achieved sustained remission up to 12 months postinfusion; IL-6 blockade reduced neuroinflammatory MRI lesions.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ongoing trials:</bold> A phase 2 pilot study at the University of Padua (NCT05724386) is currently evaluating tocilizumab in Parry-Romberg syndrome and morphea overlap syndromes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r98">[98]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>2. Neuroimaging and Pathophysiologic Correlates</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold> MRI and positron emission tomography (PET) studies</bold> Neuroimaging studies by Stone et&#x000a0;al (2019,&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Neurology</italic>) and Tolkachjov et al&#x000a0;confirmed ipsilateral white-matter lesions, leptomeningeal enhancement, and cerebral hemiatrophy in 45% to 65% of patients with Parry-Romberg syndrome and&#x000a0;neurologic symptoms. PET-CT demonstrated reduced glucose metabolism in affected hemispheres, supporting a chronic neuroinflammatory and vasculitic etiology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r99">[99]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Outcome:</bold> MRI abnormalities correlated with active cutaneous disease and seizure frequency, underscoring the role of early immunosuppression.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Optical coherence tomography angiography (OCTA)</bold> Santiago et al (2023, <italic toggle="yes">Eye</italic>) reported reduced retinal microvascular density and choroidal thickness in Parry-Romberg syndrome, correlating with disease duration and neuro-ocular involvement.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Implication:</bold> OCTA may serve as a noninvasive biomarker for disease monitoring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r100">[100]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>3. Reconstructive and Regenerative Therapies</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold> Autologous fat grafting</bold> Long-term series by Coleman and Siemionow (2017, <italic toggle="yes">Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery</italic>) demonstrated that autologous structural fat grafting offers sustained soft-tissue correction and skin texture improvement in Parry-Romberg syndrome. Fat survival improved with repeated sessions and early intervention during the quiescent phase of the disease.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Outcome:</bold> 65% to 75% volume retention at 2 years; significant psychosocial improvement</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Current focus:</bold>&#x000a0;Stromal vascular fraction and adipose-derived stem cells for regenerative enhancement are undergoing phase 1 and phase 2 clinical trials.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Free flap reconstruction</bold> Recent multicenter data&#x000a0;comparing anterolateral thigh and parascapular free flaps in 52 cases of Parry&#x02013;Romberg syndrome&#x000a0;showed superior volume stability and symmetry with anterolateral thigh flaps.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r101">[101]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Outcome:</bold> Low flap failure rate (&#x0003c;3%), improved aesthetic scores, stable symmetry up to 5 years</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Guidelines:</bold>&#x000a0;Reconstructive procedures recommended after &#x02265;12 months of disease inactivity (per European Academy of Dermatology and Venereology and the American Society of Plastic Surgeons consensus).</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Stem-cell&#x02013;enriched regenerative trials</bold> Ongoing clinical studies are exploring autologous fat grafts enriched with stem cells&#x000a0;for improved long-term results.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>NCT04217155 (US)</bold>: Evaluating stromal vascular fraction&#x02013;enhanced lipotransfer in Parry-Romberg syndrome for sustained contour correction and vascularization</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>NCT06018321 (Italy)</bold>: Phase 2 study assessing allogeneic mesenchymal stem cell therapy in neurocutaneous autoimmune atrophies (including Parry&#x02013;Romberg syndrome)<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r102">[102]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>4. Neurologic and Immunologic Research</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold> Neuroimmune interface</bold> Histopathologic studies (Marrero et al, 2020, <italic toggle="yes">Brain Pathology</italic>) confirmed perivascular T-cell infiltration and microglial activation in brain tissue and dermis of patients with Parry-Romberg syndrome, similar to Rasmussen encephalitis. Elevated IL-6 and&#x000a0;CXC motif chemokine ligand 13 (CXCL13) levels in cerebrospinal fluid correlated with neuroimaging abnormalities, supporting a shared inflammatory mechanism.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Outcome:</bold> Open-label use of IL-6 inhibitors and B-cell depletion therapy demonstrated concurrent neurologic and cutaneous improvement.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Autoantibody profiling</bold> Moran et al (2021, <italic toggle="yes">Clinical Immunology</italic>) identified ANA positivity in 52% and anti&#x02013;single-stranded DNA antibodies in 17% of patients, reinforcing the autoimmune hypothesis.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ongoing work:</bold> Whole-exome sequencing studies aim to identify shared immune-regulatory gene variants with morphea.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r103">[103]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>5. Quality-of-Life and Psychosocial Outcomes</bold>
</p>
        <p>Results from recent cross-sectional studies (Yang et&#x000a0;al, 2022,&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Dermatology</italic>) reported a significant psychosocial impact, especially among adolescents, associated with&#x000a0;facial disfigurement. The&#x000a0;Facial Assessment and Cosmetic Enhancement-Quality of Life&#x000a0;(FACE-Q) scale demonstrated marked improvement in quality of life following staged fat grafting and free-flap reconstruction.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Outcome:</bold> &#x0003e;80% reported improved social confidence and self-esteem postoperatively.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Recommendation:</bold> Integrated psychological assessment and counseling form part of the international consensus pathway.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r104">[104]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table10" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 11. Key Evidence and Trials</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
