            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Abbreviations:</italic> ADSC, adipose-derived stem cells; CXCL13,&#x000a0;CXC motif chemokine ligand 13;FDG-PET, fluorodeoxyglucose positron emission tomography; OCTA, ocular coherence tomography angiography</p>
        <p>The cumulative evidence positions Parry-Romberg Syndrome as a neuroimmunologic and vasculopathic disorder amenable to early immunosuppression followed by staged regenerative reconstruction. Emerging biologics and stem-cell&#x02013;based therapies represent the next frontier, aiming to restore not only structure but also microvascular and neural integrity. International collaborative registries and ongoing trials (eg, NCT04217155, NCT05724386) are expected to define standardized protocols and improve long-term outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s13" sec-type="Treatment Planning ">
        <title>Treatment Planning </title>
        <p>Treatment planning for Parry&#x02013;Romberg syndrome is guided by disease stage (active vs stable), extent of tissue involvement, neurologic pathology, and psychosocial needs. An interprofessional team, comprising dermatologists, neurologists, ophthalmologists, plastic and reconstructive surgeons, and psychologists, optimizes patient outcomes. The overarching goal is 2-fold, and is described in <bold>Table 12</bold>:</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Arrest disease progression</bold> through immunomodulatory therapy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Reconstruct facial symmetry and function</bold> once the disease is quiescent&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r59">[59]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table11" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 12. Disease Stage and Planning</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Stage</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Goal</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Core interventions</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Remarks</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Active/progressive stage</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Suppress inflammation and autoimmune activity</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Corticosteroids, methotrexate, mycophenolate mofetil, rituximab (in refractory disease)</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Immunotherapy initiated early reduces neurocutaneous progression</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Quiescent/stable stage</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Volume restoration and aesthetic correction</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Autologous fat grafting, dermal fillers, flap reconstructions, stem-cell&#x02013;based regenerative therapy</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Surgery delayed &#x02265;12 months after stabilization</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Stepwise Therapeutic Framework</bold>
</p>
        <p>A stepwise framework is essential to treatment planning because patients must have a stable disease before&#x000a0;surgical correction. <bold>Table 13</bold>&#x000a0;summarizes international guidelines.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Step 1: Clinical and imaging assessment</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Determine disease activity using MRI (for cerebral atrophy and leptomeningeal enhancement) and clinical progression mapping (serial photography and 3D facial scans)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Autoimmune serology (ANA, anti&#x02013;Scl-70) to confirm inflammatory activity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Use of validated activity scoring systems, such as the Localized Scleroderma Skin Severity Index&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;Disease Damage Index, to define remission&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r2">[2]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Step 2: Immunosuppressive induction</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Corticosteroids</bold>: Prednisolone 0.5 to 1 mg/kg/d for 6-8 weeks followed by taper</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Methotrexate</bold>: 15 to 25 mg/wk orally or subcutaneously, continued for 6-12 months</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Refractory disease:</bold> Mycophenolate mofetil 1&#x02013;1.5 g twice daily or azathioprine 2 mg/kg/d</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Severe neurocutaneous variant:</bold> Rituximab 1 g IV on days 1 and 15, or tocilizumab 8 mg/kg IV monthly, has shown promising results in limited trials&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r105">[105]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Adjuncts:</bold> Hydroxychloroquine 200 mg/d may stabilize mild disease</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Step 3: Monitoring and transition to maintenance</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Clinical stability:</bold>&#x000a0;No new atrophic changes for 6-12 months</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>MRI stabilization:</bold>&#x000a0;No new lesions</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gradual shift to low-dose methotrexate or mycophenolate mofetil for maintenance for 1-2 years</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Reconstructive and Regenerative Phase</bold>
</p>
        <p>Once the disease has stabilized, the focus shifts to restoring both aesthetic and functional outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r59">[59]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Soft-tissue restoration</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Autologous fat grafting (Coleman technique):</bold> Primary reconstructive option; restores contour and improves skin quality through adipose-derived stem cells</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Stromal vascular function&#x02013;enhanced lipofilling:</bold> Use of stromal vascular fraction and platelet-rich plasma enhances graft survival and neovascularization</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Synthetic fillers:</bold> Temporary correction (eg, hyaluronic acid, calcium hydroxyapatite) for mild asymmetry; not recommended in active disease</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Skeletal and structural reconstruction</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<bold>Free flaps:</bold>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p><bold>Anterolateral thigh flap:</bold> Provides stable soft tissue and contour volume; favored for extensive defects</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p><bold>Parascapular or latissimus dorsi flaps:</bold> Suitable for deep orbital and zygomatic restoration</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Mandibular bone grafting:</bold>&#x000a0;Indicated for severe bony atrophy with occlusal discrepancy&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r106">[106]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Neurological and ophthalmic management</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Antiepileptics:</bold> Carbamazepine or levetiracetam for focal seizures secondary to cortical irritation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ophthalmic management:</bold> Artificial tears, punctal plugs, and correction of enophthalmos in severe disease via orbital fat grafting</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neurovascular monitoring:</bold> Regular MRI surveillance to assess for vasculitic sequelae or cortical atrophy progression&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r59">[59]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Psychosocial and aesthetic integration</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Psychological counseling:</bold> Essential, particularly for adolescents with visible deformity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cosmetic camouflage/medical tattooing:</bold> For superficial pigmentation irregularities</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Speech and occupational therapy:</bold>&#x000a0;Indicated when perioral involvement affects function&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r107">[107]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>Table 13.</bold>&#x000a0;Interprofessional Consensus (International Guidelines)</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table12" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Organization/consensus</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Recommendations</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>European Academy of Dermatology and Venereology</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early immunosuppressive therapy with methotrexate &#x000b1; corticosteroids; delay reconstruction &#x02265;12 months after stabilization</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>American Society of Plastic Surgeons</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Prefer autologous fat or an anterolateral thigh flap for soft-tissue replacement; ensure at least 1 year of quiescence before surgery</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>European Neurology Society&#x000a0;</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>MRI and EEG are recommended for all patients with neurologic symptoms; initiate corticosteroids promptly if symptomatic</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Indian Society of Craniofacial Surgeons</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Combination medical and reconstructive therapy improves outcomes; recommend staged correction over single-stage volumetric replacement</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Abbreviations:</italic> EEG, electroencephalogram.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Personalized Treatment Algorithm</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Diagnosis confirmed</bold> &#x02192; Baseline MRI, autoimmune profile</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Active disease</bold> &#x02192; High-dose corticosteroids + methotrexate &#x000b1; biologic</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Monitor 6&#x02013;12 months</bold> &#x02192; Clinical and radiologic stability achieved</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Transition phase</bold> &#x02192; Maintenance immunotherapy (low-dose methotrexate or mycophenolate mofetil)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Stable phase</bold> &#x02192; Fat grafting/flap reconstruction/aesthetic correction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Rehabilitation</bold> &#x02192; Psychological support, ocular care, and speech therapy</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Emerging and Future Directions</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>3D bioprinting</bold> for customized facial implants</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Gene-expression profiling</bold> to identify early biomarkers of activity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Exosome-rich adipose-derived stem cell therapy</bold>&#x000a0;is showing promise in tissue regeneration</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Artificial intelligence&#x02013;based facial symmetry mapping</bold> for objective reconstruction planning&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r108">[108]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s14" sec-type="Toxicity and Adverse Effect Management">
        <title>Toxicity and Adverse Effect Management</title>
        <p>Management of Parry-Romberg Syndrome often requires prolonged immunosuppressive and reconstructive therapy, both of which may carry significant adverse effects. The clinician&#x02019;s role extends beyond treating facial atrophy to ensuring systemic safety, early detection of drug-related toxicity, and interprofessional follow-up to mitigate secondary complications. <bold>Table 14</bold>&#x000a0;summarizes treatment-related toxicities (see <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Adverse Effects of Parry-Romberg Syndrome Therapy).</p>
        <p>
<bold> Immunosuppressive Toxicities</bold> The mainstay of medical therapy for active Parry&#x02013;Romberg syndrome includes corticosteroids, methotrexate, mycophenolate mofetil, cyclophosphamide, and biologics such as rituximab or tocilizumab.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Corticosteroids</bold> may induce hypertension, hyperglycemia, osteoporosis, mood changes, and immunosuppression. Tapering schedules, calcium and vitamin D supplementation, and bone-density monitoring are recommended.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Methotrexate</bold> can cause hepatotoxicity, bone marrow suppression, and pulmonary fibrosis; liver function tests and complete blood counts must be monitored every 4-8 weeks. Folate supplementation (1-5 mg/d) minimizes toxicity.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Mycophenolate mofetil</bold> may cause gastrointestinal distress and leukopenia; dose adjustments are guided by white blood cell counts.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cyclophosphamide</bold> carries risks of hemorrhagic cystitis, infertility, and malignancy. Regular urinalysis and hydration protocols, along with mesna prophylaxis to prevent bladder toxicity, are indicated.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Biologic agents</bold> (rituximab, tocilizumab) can precipitate infusion reactions and increase susceptibility to infections. Tuberculosis and hepatitis B screening are mandatory before initiation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r109">[109]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Surgical and Reconstructive Complications</bold> In the stable phase, interventions such as autologous fat grafting, dermal fillers, and free-flap reconstruction restore facial symmetry.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Fat graft resorption</bold> occurs in 20% to 40% of patients, necessitating repeat procedures.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Infection or hematoma</bold>&#x000a0;risk is minimized with perioperative antibiotics and gentle tissue handling.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Flap necrosis</bold> and <bold>vascular thrombosis</bold> may occur in microvascular reconstructions; postoperative Doppler surveillance is essential.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Stem-cell&#x02013;based therapies</bold>, though promising, pose potential risks of immune rejection, ectopic tissue formation, or theoretical oncogenicity; long-term surveillance and ethical oversight are mandatory.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r110">[110]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Neurologic and Ophthalmic Adverse Effects</bold> Neuroimmunologic involvement may predispose patients to seizures, headaches, or white-matter lesions. Antiepileptic therapy can produce fatigue, mood alterations, or hepatotoxicity. Corticosteroids can cause glaucoma, cataract, or corneal thinning, necessitating periodic slit-lamp and intraocular pressure evaluation.</p>
        <p>
<bold> Psychosocial and Systemic Toxicities</bold> Chronic disfigurement and recurrent interventions often lead to depression, anxiety, or body-image disturbances. Early referral to a psychologist or psychiatrist improves adherence and coping. Nutritional counseling, physiotherapy, and stress-reduction programs (such as yoga and mindfulness meditation) contribute to overall well-being.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r111">[111]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r112">[112]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table13" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 14. Common Therapeutic Agents and Associated Toxicities in Parry&#x02013;Romberg Syndrome</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
