        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Abbreviations:</italic> CBC, complete blood cell count; DEXA, dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry; LFTs, liver function tests; GI, gastrointestinal; HBV, hepatitis B virus; IL, interleukin; TB, tuberculosis</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s15" sec-type="Staging">
        <title>Staging</title>
        <p>The currently used&#x000a0;classification system&#x000a0;for Parry-Romberg syndrome cases is based on the extent of trigeminal nerve distribution involvement:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Mild:&#x000a0;</bold>Skin or subcutaneous fat atrophy limited to a single division of the trigeminal nerve</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Moderate:</bold> Skin or fat atrophy involving&#x000a0;2 branches of the trigeminal territory</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Severe:</bold> Atrophy&#x000a0;in all 3 divisions of the trigeminal nerve or any bony atrophy&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r113">[113]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Parry-Romberg Syndrome follows a characteristic yet variable progression that can be broadly categorized into&#x000a0;active,&#x000a0;burnout&#x000a0;(stable), and reconstructive stages. Although no universally accepted international staging system exists, several clinical frameworks have been proposed to guide diagnosis, management, and surgical timing. The disease often evolves over 2 to 10 years before stabilizing, though late reactivation has been reported (see <bold>Image</bold><bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Parry-Romberg Syndrome, Stages).&#x000a0;<bold>Table 15 </bold>summarizes&#x000a0;a clinical staging system from existing clinical models.</p>
        <p>
<bold>1. Early or Active Inflammatory Stage</bold>
</p>
        <p>This stage is characterized by progressive hemifacial atrophy, often beginning with subtle skin discoloration or tightness, followed by loss of subcutaneous tissue. Clinical findings include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Localized skin induration resembling morphea</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypopigmentation or hyperpigmentation along the affected side</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trigeminal neuralgia, headaches, or migraine-like pain</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ipsilateral alopecia, madarosis, or enophthalmos</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Histopathologically, this stage is characterized by&#x000a0;perivascular lymphocytic infiltration, dermal fibrosis, and neurovasculitis. MRI may reveal white-matter hyperintensities and ipsilateral cerebral hemiatrophy. Immunosuppressive therapy (methotrexate &#x000b1; corticosteroids) is most effective in halting progression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r47">[47]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>2. Atrophic or Stable Stage</bold>
</p>
        <p>Once the active inflammatory phase subsides, the disease enters a quiescent&#x000a0;phase that can&#x000a0;last&#x000a0;for years. This stage is marked by:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Complete cessation of active atrophy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Persistent facial asymmetry and skeletal remodeling</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Loss of subcutaneous fat and muscle bulk without further progression</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ocular involvement (eg, enophthalmos, uveitis) may persist</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Biopsy reveals fibrotic, avascular tissue with minimal inflammatory infiltration. Radiographically, bone hypoplasia of the maxilla, mandible, and zygoma may be evident. This stage offers a window of opportunity for reconstructive surgery, during which techniques such as autologous fat grafting, dermal fillers, or free tissue transfer can yield optimal outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r112">[112]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>3. Late or Reconstructive Stage</bold>
</p>
        <p>In this phase, deformity has stabilized, and secondary reconstructive procedures are indicated. The goals include restoring symmetry, promoting psychosocial rehabilitation, and preventing functional complications. Key clinical signs:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Stable soft tissue and bony changes for &#x02265;2 years</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Absence of inflammatory activity on MRI</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Facial asymmetry involving the orbit, mandible, and nose</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Occasional recurrence of autoimmune activity (rare)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Interventions include autologous fat grafting, stem-cell&#x02013;enriched lipofilling, or microvascular free flaps (eg, anterolateral thigh flap). Dental and orthodontic correction may also be required for jaw deformities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r114">[114]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table14" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 15. Proposed Clinical Staging System (Composite Adaptation)</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Stage</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Clinical features</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Investigations</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Therapeutic implications</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 61.899px;">
                <td style="height: 61.899px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Stage I: active (inflammatory)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 61.899px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early discoloration, pain, neuralgia, progressive atrophy</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 61.899px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>MRI brain/orbit, serology, biopsy showing lymphocytic infiltration</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 61.899px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Initiate immunosuppressants (methotrexate, corticosteroids)</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 61px;">
                <td style="height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Stage II: atrophic (stable)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Soft-tissue and bone loss stabilized, with minimal inflammation</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>MRI showing fibrosis, absent enhancement</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Surgical planning for contour correction</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 61px;">
                <td style="height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Stage III: reconstructive (postatrophy)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Residual deformity, no disease activity</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Imaging stable &#x02265;2 years</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Fat grafting, flap reconstruction, and stem-cell therapy</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s16" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Parry-Romberg syndrome progresses to the burnout phase within 2 to 20 years of the initial active disease. Facial asymmetry is often significant, with gross disfigurement. Ocular manifestations ranging from uveitis to keratitis to retinal pathologies require active management. Repeated attacks of uveitis or retinal vasculitis can lead to secondary glaucoma with variable visual prognosis.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Neurological manifestations are often well-controlled and cause minimal disability. The prognosis of Parry-Romberg syndrome is highly variable and depends on the extent, activity, and timing of disease progression, as well as the presence of neurologic and ophthalmologic involvement. Parry&#x02013;Romberg syndrome typically follows a self-limiting but progressive, monophasic course, in which the disease gradually advances over several years before reaching a stable phase. In most patients, progression ceases spontaneously within 2 to 10 years of onset; however, late reactivation has been reported in approximately&#x000a0;10% to 15%&#x000a0;of patients, particularly in those with autoimmune overlap syndromes,&#x000a0;such as localized scleroderma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r115">[115]</xref>&#x000a0;<bold>Table 16</bold> lists the favorable and nonfavorable prognostic indicators.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Natural Course</bold>
</p>
        <p>In the early active stage, Parry-Romberg syndrome exhibits unpredictable activity, characterized by fluctuating phases of soft-tissue and bony resorption. Once the disease stabilizes, further progression is rare but can occur with new autoimmune triggers or trauma. The severity of atrophy ranges from mild subcutaneous loss to severe hemifacial disfigurement involving bone, muscle, and neurovascular structures. The natural course tends to stabilize earlier in children but may result in more severe facial asymmetry due to interference with craniofacial development.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Functional Prognosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>While primarily a cosmetic and reconstructive challenge, Parry&#x02013;Romberg syndrome carries significant functional implications:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ocular outcomes </bold>depend on early recognition and management of complications such as enophthalmos, exposure keratopathy, uveitis, or secondary glaucoma. With timely therapy, the visual prognosis remains favorable in greater than 80% of patients.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neurologic prognosis</bold>&#x000a0;varies by involvement; patients with associated epilepsy, trigeminal neuralgia, or white-matter lesions have higher morbidity but rarely life-threatening outcomes. Seizure control is usually achieved with standard antiepileptic therapy, although refractory epilepsy&#x000a0;occurs&#x000a0;in 5% to 10% of patients.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Dental and maxillofacial outcomes:</bold>&#x000a0;Influenced by the timing of reconstruction; delayed intervention can lead to malocclusion, mandibular hypoplasia, and persistent asymmetry.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r116">[116]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Psychosocial and Developmental Impact</bold>
</p>
        <p>Parry&#x02013;Romberg syndrome exerts profound psychosocial effects due to progressive disfigurement during childhood and adolescence. Depression, social withdrawal, and anxiety are common, particularly in pediatric and adolescent populations. Early psychological counseling and reconstructive planning improve self-esteem and long-term quality of life. Results from studies report that up to 70% of patients experience moderate to severe psychosocial distress, underscoring the need for interprofessional management.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Response to Therapy and Long-Term Outcomes</bold>
</p>
        <p>Early initiation of immunomodulatory therapy (methotrexate, mycophenolate mofetil, or corticosteroids) during the inflammatory phase can halt or slow disease progression in 60% to 80% of patients. Biologics, such as&#x000a0;rituximab and tocilizumab,&#x000a0;have shown promise in treating&#x000a0;refractory or recurrent disease, improving both cutaneous and neurological manifestations. Once the disease becomes inactive, reconstructive procedures (autologous fat grafting, dermal fillers, microvascular free flaps) provide durable cosmetic restoration with good functional and aesthetic outcomes. Fat graft resorption may occur, necessitating repeated sessions; however, results are generally stable over 5 to 10 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r117">[117]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table15" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 16. Prognostic Indicators</title>
          </caption>
          <table>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Favorable prognostic factors</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Poor prognostic factors</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early diagnosis and immunotherapy during the active phase</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Delayed diagnosis with established fibrosis</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Limited soft-tissue involvement</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Neurologic complications (seizures, white-matter lesions)</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Absence of autoimmune comorbidities</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Overlap with linear scleroderma or systemic autoimmune disease</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Timely reconstructive surgery after stabilization</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ocular complications (uveitis, glaucoma, enophthalmos)</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Pediatric onset with short disease duration</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Recurrent or relapsing course after therapy</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Long-Term Outlook</bold>
</p>
        <p>With appropriate interprofessional care, the life expectancy of patients with Parry-Romberg syndrome remains normal. The overall prognosis is favorable in mild to moderate disease and guarded in severe or multisystemic disease. Patients with early-onset disease, intracranial vascular malformations, or coexisting autoimmune disorders require long-term monitoring. Advances in stem-cell&#x02013;assisted reconstruction, autologous fat grafting, and 3D craniofacial modeling have significantly improved aesthetic outcomes and functional rehabilitation.</p>
        <p>In summary, Parry-Romberg syndrome is a chronic, self-limiting neurocutaneous disorder with variable severity. The prognosis is excellent, but the morbidity is high due to facial disfigurement, neurologic complications, and psychosocial burden. Early identification, immunosuppressive control during the active phase, and timely reconstructive surgery in the stable phase offer the best chances for functional recovery and psychosocial well-being.&#x000a0;Interprofessional collaboration among ophthalmologists, neurologists, dermatologists, and reconstructive surgeons remains pivotal in optimizing long-term outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r118">[118]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s17" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Although Parry&#x02013;Romberg syndrome primarily affects facial soft tissue, it can also lead to ophthalmic and neurologic complications that contribute substantially to morbidity. These complications may arise during either the active inflammatory phase or the late quiescent stage due to chronic neurovascular and connective tissue damage. The complications that can manifest with Parry&#x02013;Romberg syndrome are listed in <bold>Table 17</bold> and are as follows:</p>
        <p>
<bold>Ophthalmic Complications</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Corneal opacity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Glaucoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Restrictive strabismus</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Retinal detachment</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Loss of vision</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Phthisis bulbi or atrophic bulbi</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Contracted socket</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Neurologic Complications</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Recurrent seizure and status epilepticus</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Subarachnoid hemorrhage from rupture of cerebral aneurysms&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r15">[15]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Spontaneous carotid-jugular fistula and carotid dissection due to cerebral vascular malformations</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cerebral infarction from focal stenosis of the cerebral vasculature</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-139206.table16" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 17.&#x000a0;&#x000a0;Complications of Parry-Romberg syndrome</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>System/region affected</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Specific complications</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Clinical description/impact</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Management/prognostic implication</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Dermatologic</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Progressive facial atrophy</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Loss of subcutaneous fat, skin tightening, and pigmentation changes</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Immunosuppressive therapy during the active phase; reconstructive surgery after stabilization</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Ophthalmic</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Enophthalmos</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Due to orbital fat atrophy, causing a sunken globe appearance</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Orbital implants or fat grafting to restore volume</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Exposure keratopathy</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Secondary to enophthalmos and eyelid retraction</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Lubricants, tarsorrhaphy, or reconstructive correction</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Uveitis and retinal vasculitis</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Chronic inflammation leading to visual loss</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Topical or systemic corticosteroids and immunosuppression</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Secondary glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Due to chronic inflammation or steroid response</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Antiglaucoma medications or surgical intervention</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Neurologic</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Trigeminal neuralgia</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Facial pain along the trigeminal distribution</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Carbamazepine, gabapentin, or nerve blocks</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Epilepsy</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Focal seizures are often ipsilateral to atrophy</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Antiepileptic therapy; MRI monitoring for white-matter lesions</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hemiparesis and migraine</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Associated with cerebral vasculitis or atrophy</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Immunotherapy and vasodilators; neurologic follow-up</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Maxillofacial/dental</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Jaw and tooth deformities</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hypoplasia of the mandible, malocclusion, and delayed tooth eruption</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Orthodontic or orthognathic surgery after disease stabilization</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Malocclusion and deviation of the mouth</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Resulting from unilateral muscle and bone atrophy</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Corrective dental alignment or reconstructive procedures</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Musculoskeletal</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Skeletal hypoplasia</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Facial and cranial asymmetry due to underdevelopment</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Surgical correction post disease stabilization</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Vascular</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Intracranial aneurysms/malformations</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Rare; due to neurovasculitis and endothelial damage</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Regular neuroimaging and vascular monitoring</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Autoimmune/systemic</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Coexistent morphea or scleroderma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Skin thickening and fibrosis extending beyond the face</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Systemic immunosuppressants (methotrexate, mycophenolate mofetil)</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Thyroiditis, lupus, or vitiligo</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Autoimmune overlap syndromes</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Targeted systemic therapy as per comorbidity</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Psychological/developmental</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Depression, anxiety, body dysmorphia</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Due to disfigurement and social stigma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Counseling, psychiatric support, reconstructive surgery</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Social isolation and low self-esteem</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Especially in adolescents</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early psychosocial support, patient education</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Postoperative</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Fat graft resorption</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Common after reconstructive interventions</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Repeat grafting or use of stem-cell&#x02013;enriched fat</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 132px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;</td>
                <td style="width: 154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Flap failure or infection</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.108px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Rare but significant reconstructive complication</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 195.892px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early recognition and revision surgery</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s18" sec-type="Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care">
        <title>Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care</title>
        <p>Postoperative and rehabilitative care in Parry-Romberg syndrome requires an interprofessional, long-term approach aimed at optimizing aesthetic restoration, preserving functional outcomes, and addressing the psychosocial sequelae of facial disfigurement. Because Parry-Romberg syndrome often stabilizes after several years of progression, surgical and rehabilitative interventions are ideally undertaken only after the disease has reached its inactive phase to minimize recurrence and postoperative tissue loss.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Immediate Postoperative Care</bold>
</p>
        <p>After reconstructive procedures such as autologous fat grafting, dermal fillers, free-flap transfers, or osteotomies, patients require close monitoring for graft viability, infection, and contour irregularities. Pain control, head elevation, and pressure avoidance at the operative site are essential during the early recovery period. Broad-spectrum antibiotics and anti-inflammatory medications are often prescribed to prevent secondary infections and excessive postoperative edema. In microsurgical flaps, regular assessment of color, temperature, and capillary refill helps identify early vascular compromise. Autologous fat graft recipients should avoid strenuous activity for&#x000a0;2 weeks to allow neovascularization and graft stabilization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r119">[119]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Ocular and Neurologic Follow-Up</bold>
</p>
        <p>Ophthalmologic care is integral because ocular complications such as enophthalmos, exposure keratopathy, uveitis, or secondary glaucoma may persist or worsen after facial reconstruction. Frequent follow-up with slit-lamp biomicroscopy, intraocular pressure measurement, and fundus examination is advised. Neurologic monitoring remains essential for patients with prior trigeminal neuralgia, epilepsy, or white-matter lesions, with adjustment of anticonvulsant or immunomodulatory medications as needed. MRI surveillance at 6- to 12-month intervals is recommended for patients with cerebral involvement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r120">[120]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Physical and Functional Rehabilitation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Physical therapy plays an important role in restoring facial muscle tone and symmetry. Early postoperative facial physiotherapy, including massage, neuromuscular retraining, and mirror-guided exercises, helps reduce edema, prevent contracture formation, and improve the&#x000a0;mobility of the reconstructed tissues. Speech and swallowing therapy may be indicated for patients with significant mandibular or perioral involvement to restore normal oral function. Dental rehabilitation through orthodontic correction, occlusal adjustment, or prosthetic restoration is crucial for maintaining long-term masticatory balance and optimal aesthetics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r121">[121]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Psychosocial and Emotional Support</bold>
</p>
        <p>Psychological rehabilitation plays a critical role in the postoperative continuum. Because Parry-Romberg syndrome often manifests during childhood or adolescence, body-image concerns, anxiety, and social withdrawal are common. Structured counseling, peer-support programs, and involvement of clinical psychologists or psychiatrists can markedly improve emotional well-being. Educational counseling should also be provided to parents and caregivers to ensure sustained social reintegration and support. Cognitive-behavioral therapy and support groups for rare craniofacial disorders have demonstrated measurable benefits in quality-of-life indices.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r121">[121]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Long-Term Maintenance and Secondary Procedures</bold>
</p>
        <p>Reconstructive outcomes must be reassessed periodically, as fat graft resorption or volume asymmetry may occur in up to 40% of patients within 12 to 24 months. Secondary corrections such as repeat grafting, scar revision, or the use of stem-cell&#x02013;enriched adipose tissue may be required to maintain contour stability. Patients undergoing osseous reconstruction should be evaluated annually with 3D imaging to assess graft integration and bone growth, especially in pediatric patients. Immunosuppressive therapy should be gradually tapered under rheumatologic supervision once the disease has remained quiescent for at least 12 to 18 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r122">[122]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Nutritional and Lifestyle Considerations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Nutritional optimization promotes wound healing and tissue regeneration. Diets rich in protein, antioxidants, and omega-3 fatty acids are encouraged. Smoking cessation is strongly advised, as nicotine impairs microvascular circulation and graft survival. Adequate hydration and vitamin D supplementation further support postoperative recovery and bone metabolism.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Rehabilitation Outcome Goals</bold>
</p>
        <p>The ultimate goal of postoperative and rehabilitative care in Parry-Romberg syndrome is to restore facial symmetry, preserve visual and neurologic function, and ensure psychological recovery. With coordinated interprofessional care, more than 80% of patients have satisfactory cosmetic and functional outcomes, as documented in long-term follow-up studies. Early integration of physiotherapy, psychological support, and reconstructive planning remains the cornerstone of successful rehabilitation in Parry-Romberg syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r123">[123]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s19" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Management of&#x000a0;Parry&#x02013;Romberg Syndrome requires an interprofessional&#x000a0;consultation framework, as the disease affects multiple systems, including dermatologic, neurologic, ophthalmologic, dental, maxillofacial, and psychological domains. Optimal care hinges on early diagnosis, comprehensive evaluation, and coordinated interspecialty collaboration to minimize deformity progression and optimize rehabilitation outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold> Neurology Consultation</bold>
</p>
        <p>A neurology consult is essential for all patients with neurologic manifestations such as trigeminal neuralgia, epilepsy, hemifacial pain, or focal neurologic deficits. Neurologists evaluate for seizure foci using MRI or EEG, identify white-matter changes, and monitor for intracranial vascular malformations. Immunomodulatory therapy may be initiated if imaging suggests neuroinflammation or vasculitic changes. Long-term follow-up prevents progression to&#x000a0;Rasmussen encephalitis-like syndromes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r124">[124]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Ophthalmology Consultation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Given the high frequency of ocular involvement, including enophthalmos, uveitis, scleroderma-like keratitis, and exposure keratopathy, ophthalmology evaluation is vital. Detailed slit-lamp, fundus, and orbital imaging should be performed to document ocular surface integrity, intraocular pressure, and retinal changes. Regular monitoring&#x000a0;helps detect early secondary glaucoma or retinal vasculitis. Coordination with oculoplastic surgeons is recommended for orbital volume augmentation and eyelid correction to prevent vision-threatening complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r125">[125]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Dermatology and Rheumatology Consultation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Dermatologists play a central role in assessing the cutaneous phase of the disease, particularly during its active progression, marked by induration, pigmentation, or morphea-like overlap.&#x000a0;A rheumatology consultation is recommended to screen for&#x000a0;autoimmune overlap syndromes,&#x000a0;such as systemic sclerosis, lupus, or rheumatoid arthritis. In the active stage, methotrexate (15-25 mg/wk) and systemic corticosteroids remain first-line therapy, guided by rheumatologic supervision. Collaboration between dermatology and rheumatology ensures effective disease stabilization before reconstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r125">[125]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery Consultation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Plastic surgery evaluation is indicated once the disease reaches a stable phase (typically after 2-10 years). The reconstructive surgeon assesses the extent of soft tissue, bone, and muscle atrophy using 3D imaging, MRI, and volumetric scans. Depending on the&#x000a0;extent of the deficit, procedures such as&#x000a0;autologous fat grafting, dermal-fat transfer, or microvascular free flaps (parascapular, latissimus dorsi, or groin flaps)&#x000a0;may be&#x000a0;considered. Surgeons should coordinate timing with dermatology and rheumatology to ensure remission before undertaking definitive interventions. See StatPearls' companion reference, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/81676">"Photorefractive Keratectomy,"</ext-link> for further information.</p>
        <p>
<bold> Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery/Dental Consultation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Maxillofacial surgeons and orthodontists evaluate jaw asymmetry, malocclusion, and dental maldevelopment due to facial bone hypoplasia. Early detection of dental crowding, crossbite, or mandibular deviation allows timely orthodontic correction. Reconstructive osteotomy or distraction osteogenesis may be performed in adults, while pediatric patients require sequential monitoring to minimize growth interference. Collaboration with prosthodontists is essential for restoring occlusion and esthetics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r126">[126]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Psychiatry and Psychology Consultation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Psychological evaluation is indispensable given the psychosocial burden of facial disfigurement, particularly among adolescents and young adults. Depression, anxiety, and body dysmorphia are common and may hinder social integration. Psychiatrists provide pharmacologic therapy when required, while clinical psychologists administer cognitive-behavioral therapy, self-image training, and social reintegration programs. Group therapy and peer support improve treatment compliance and self-esteem.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r127">[127]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Radiology and Neuroradiology Consultation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Radiologists&#x000a0;document the extent, progression, and intracranial&#x000a0;involvement&#x000a0;using MRI and CT. Typical findings include white-matter hyperintensities, leptomeningeal enhancement, and cortical atrophy ipsilateral to the facial changes. Radiologic surveillance every 6 to 12 months helps guide neurologic management and determine the timing of reconstructive procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r128">[128]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Physical Therapy and Speech Therapy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Physical therapists assist in facial reanimation, promoting symmetry and muscular retraining after reconstruction. Techniques include biofeedback, mirror therapy, and electrical stimulation. Speech therapy addresses articulation and swallowing dysfunction resulting from oral asymmetry. Early rehabilitation is associated with better long-term functional and aesthetic outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r129">[129]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Nutrition and Endocrinology Consultation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Nutrition specialists ensure adequate protein and micronutrient intake for tissue healing. In selected patients with thyroiditis or autoimmune endocrinopathies, an endocrinology consultation is warranted to manage hormonal imbalances. Vitamin D, calcium, and omega-3 fatty acid supplementation are recommended during the recovery period.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Interprofessional Board Discussion</bold>
</p>
        <p>Complex cases benefit from an interprofessional board for Parry-Romberg syndrome comprising neurologists, ophthalmologists, plastic surgeons, dermatologists, rheumatologists, and psychologists. Such collaborative decision-making aligns medical stabilization with surgical planning, thereby improving long-term outcomes. Consensus-based management protocols,&#x000a0;similar to those used in systemic sclerosis and craniofacial reconstruction programs, have been associated with higher patient satisfaction and improved functional outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r130">[130]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s20" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>The onset of Parry&#x02013;Romberg syndrome is marked by the appearance of pigmentary lesions on the neck and face, followed by progressive facial asymmetry and neurologic and ocular symptoms. Recent advances have improved early diagnosis and led to more favorable cosmetic outcomes for patients. Early recognition of Parry-Romberg syndrome is essential to mitigate irreversible tissue atrophy and functional morbidity. Patients and caregivers should be educated that Parry-Romberg syndrome is a rare, typically self-limiting but progressive condition that affects one side of the face, often during the first or second decade of life. The primary goal of patient education is to raise awareness of early warning signs, such as facial asymmetry, skin tightening, and localized alopecia, and to prompt timely referral to specialists in neurology, ophthalmology, dermatology, and plastic reconstructive surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients should be reassured that, although no definitive cure exists, early interprofessional management can slow the progression and optimize both cosmetic and functional outcomes. Education must emphasize adherence to medical therapy (eg, immunosuppressants, corticosteroids, or methotrexate) and the importance of regular follow-up to monitor disease activity and neurological involvement, including seizures or trigeminal neuralgia. Counseling on psychological support is crucial, as facial disfigurement may lead to emotional distress, anxiety, and social withdrawal. Support groups and rehabilitation services should be recommended to aid in coping and restoring self-image. Parents and caregivers of pediatric patients must be counseled regarding the potential need for staged reconstructive procedures once the disease stabilizes. Finally, deterrence involves awareness among clinicians (particularly pediatricians, dermatologists, and dental surgeons) to differentiate Parry-Romberg syndrome from linear scleroderma, craniofacial microsomia, or posttraumatic atrophy. Timely intervention and patient-centered education can prevent progression-related disability and improve long-term quality of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r38">[38]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s21" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>
<bold>Diagnostic Nuances and Early Recognition</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Parry-Romberg Syndrome often begins insidiously with cutaneous discoloration, localized induration, or mild facial asymmetry, which may be mistaken for posttraumatic or idiopathic facial atrophy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Early neuroimaging (contrast-enhanced MRI) and autoimmune screening (ANA, erythrocyte sedimentation rate, and C-reactive protein) are crucial for identifying the active inflammatory stage before irreversible tissue damage occurs.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Overlap with linear scleroderma <italic toggle="yes">en coup de sabre</italic> can complicate the diagnosis; however, Parry-Romberg syndrome typically extends deeper, involving subcutaneous fat, muscle, and bone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r59">[59]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Clinical Pearls in Assessment</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>A hallmark feature is progressive hemifacial atrophy, accompanied by ipsilateral enophthalmos, mandibular shortening, and muscle wasting.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Neurologic symptoms such as seizures, migraine-like headaches, or trigeminal neuralgia precede visible atrophy in up to one-third of patients.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ocular signs (uveitis, keratitis, Horner syndrome, or retinal vasculitis)&#x000a0;require regular ophthalmic surveillance, even if asymptomatic initially.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Disease stabilization often occurs after 5-10 years, but late reactivation can occur following significant stress, trauma, or pregnancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r38">[38]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Key Pitfalls in Management</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Initiating surgical or reconstructive intervention during the active inflammatory phase can precipitate graft failure and recurrence of atrophy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Overreliance on corticosteroids without immunomodulator therapy (eg, methotrexate, mycophenolate mofetil) can lead to transient improvement but subsequent relapse.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Misdiagnosis as a purely cosmetic or dermatologic condition leads to delayed systemic evaluation and worsens functional outcomes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Neglecting dental and maxillofacial correction in growing children results in irreversible occlusal and skeletal deformities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r60">[60]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Preventive and Prognostic Insights</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>There is no known preventive strategy; however, early detection of active inflammatory lesions via imaging and prompt initiation of immunosuppressive therapy can limit progression.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Avoidance of unnecessary facial trauma, infections, and cold exposure may help reduce local vasospasm and tissue ischemia.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The long-term prognosis depends on the extent of the disease and neurologic involvement; patients without cerebral lesions typically have excellent functional outcomes after reconstruction.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>In children, growth-related asymmetry should be anticipated, and&#x000a0;serial photographic and radiographic monitoring&#x000a0;is recommended every 6-12 months until skeletal maturity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r2">[2]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Interprofessional Coordination</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Successful outcomes depend on an&#x000a0;interprofessional&#x000a0;approach involving dermatology, rheumatology, plastic surgery, neurology, ophthalmology, and psychology.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Disease quiescence must be confirmed for at least 12-18 months before any reconstructive procedure.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Use of autologous fat grafts enriched with adipose-derived stem cells improves long-term graft retention and may reduce recurrence rates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r8">[8]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Advances and Emerging Frontiers</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Stem&#x02013;cell&#x02013;based therapies, including autologous adipose-derived mesenchymal stem cells and platelet-rich plasma, have shown promise in enhancing facial volume and improving skin texture.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>3D&#x000a0;facial imaging and computer-assisted reconstruction&#x000a0;enable&#x000a0;precise volumetric planning and improved symmetry outcomes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ongoing research into neuroimmunologic mechanisms may redefine Parry-Romberg syndrome as part of the autoimmune-neurovascular spectrum rather than a distinct scleroderma subtype.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r7">[7]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Prognostic Pearls</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The absence of systemic or neurologic manifestations predicts a benign course with excellent reconstructive outcomes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The presence of seizures, intracranial vascular malformations, or extensive morphea overlap correlates with a poorer prognosis and higher relapse risk.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Psychological resilience and family support are significant determinants of long-term quality of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r26">[26]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Key Takeaway for Clinicians</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Stabilize before you reconstruct.</italic> The cornerstone of Parry-Romberg syndrome management remains timely diagnosis, early immunologic control, and delayed but precise reconstruction guided by disease quiescence and interprofessional consensus.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-139206.s22" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Parry-Romberg syndrome, or progressive hemifacial atrophy, represents a rare, multifactorial neurocutaneous disorder characterized by unilateral atrophy of facial skin, subcutaneous tissue, muscle, and bone. The condition typically manifests during the first or second decade of life and progresses slowly before stabilizing. The pathogenesis involves autoimmune, neurogenic, and vascular mechanisms, and systemic manifestations often include neurological, ophthalmic, dental, and dermatologic complications. Because Parry-Romberg syndrome may first present in ophthalmology, neurology, or dermatology clinics, timely recognition and coordinated management are essential to minimize deformity, preserve function, and improve psychosocial outcomes.</p>
        <p>Optimal management of Parry-Romberg syndrome depends on comprehensive interprofessional collaboration and patient-centered coordination across specialties. Clinicians, nurses, pharmacists, and rehabilitation specialists each play critical roles in guiding care through the disease&#x02019;s active and stable phases. Dermatologists and rheumatologists initiate immunomodulatory therapy, while neurologists manage seizures and neuroimaging surveillance. Plastic surgeons, ophthalmologists, and maxillofacial surgeons address reconstructive and ocular complications once inflammation stabilizes. Physical therapists&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;speech pathologists support functional rehabilitation, helping patients regain facial mobility, symmetry, and articulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Nurses monitor&#x000a0;treatment response&#x000a0;and wound care, while pharmacists ensure safe pharmacologic management. Psychologists and counselors provide essential psychosocial support, helping individuals develop&#x000a0;coping skills and resilience.</p>
        <p>Effective communication through shared electronic health records, protocols, and interprofessional rounds ensures continuity, patient safety, and informed decision-making.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-139206.r6">[6]</xref> Ethical transparency regarding disease unpredictability, reconstructive timing, and aesthetic outcomes enhances patient trust and engagement. Continuous education, research collaboration, and outcome audits strengthen clinician competence and improve long-term functional, aesthetic, and emotional recovery for individuals affected by Parry-Romberg syndrome.</p>
      </sec>
